tomography imaging in paediatric cardiology: a joint expert consensus document of the EACVI, ESCR, AEPC, and ESPR. Eur Heart J Cardiovasc Imaging. 2022;23(8):e279–e289. doi: 10.1093/ehjci/jeac048. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Uhlig J, Al-Bourini O, Salgado R et al (2020) Gadolinium-based contrast agents for cardiac MRI: use of linear and macrocyclic agents with associated safety profile from 154 779 European Patients. Radiol Cardiothorac Imaging 2(5):e200102. 10.1148/ryct.2020200102 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 18. Weikert T, Francone M, Abbara S, et al. Machine learning in cardiovascular radiology: ESCR position statement on design requirements, quality assessment, current applications, opportunities, and challenges. Eur Radiol. 2021;31(6):3909–3922. doi: 10.1007/s00330-020-07417-0. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Beitzke D, Salgado R, Francone M, et al. Cardiac imaging procedures and the COVID-19 pandemic: recommendations of the European Society of Cardiovascular Radiology (ESCR) Int J Cardiovasc Imaging. 2020;36(10):1801–1810. doi: 10.1007/s10554-020-01892-8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Uhlig J, Lücke C, Vliegenthart R et al (2019) Acute adverse events in cardiac MR imaging with gadolinium-based contrast agents: results from the European Society of Cardiovascular Radiology (ESCR) MRCT Registry in 72,839 patients. Eur Radiol 29(7):3686–3695. 10.1007/s00330-019-06171-2 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 21. Nensa F, Bamberg F, Rischpler C, et al. Hybrid cardiac imaging using PET/MRI: a joint position statement by the European Society of Cardiovascular Radiology (ESCR) and the European Association of Nuclear Medicine (EANM) Eur Radiol. 2018;28(10):4086–4101. doi: 10.1007/s00330-017-5008-4. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Gimelli A, Achenbach S, Buechel RR, et al. Strategies for radiation dose reduction in nuclear cardiology and cardiac computed tomography imaging: a report from the European Association of Cardiovascular Imaging (EACVI), the Cardiovascular Committee of European Association of Nuclear Medicine (EANM), and the European Society of Cardiovascular Radiology (ESCR) Eur Heart J. 2018;39(4):286–296. doi: 10.1093/eurheartj/ehx582. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Saba L, Loewe C, Weikert R et al (2022) State of the art imaging and assessment of carotid artery disease: standardization of scanning protocols, measurements and reporting - a consensus document by the European Society of Cardiovascular Radiology (ESCR). Eur Radiol. 10.1007/s00330-022-09024-7 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 24. Saba L, Loewe C, Weikert R et al (2022) State of the art imaging and assessment of carotid artery disease: the reporting - a consensus document by the European Society of Cardiovascular Radiology (ESCR). Eur Radiol (accepted for publication). 10.1007/s00330-022-09025-6',29,'2025-10-15 10:07:05','2025-10-15 10:07:05');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4666,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11436584','The American College of Medical Toxicology Annual Scientific Meeting: A Look Back and the Path Forward.','Mazer-Amirshahi M, Wax P, Dunavin A, Mycyk MB, Nelson LS.','2024','favorites shark conundrums nacct fixture locals keynotes plenaries registrants scottsdale pithy clearwater jmt rebranded clearwater diversify intervened diversify keynotes plenaries winner ellenhorn shark reconfigured fundings spadafora galvanize tireless allowable inspirational calello kathy kopec wiggins tricia steffey incalculable dunavin amirshahi kopec stolbach toce goldfine mazer amirshahi meyn','Abstract More than 20 years that have elapsed since the inaugural American College of Medical Toxicology Spring Conference in 2002. During this time, the now ACMT Annual Scientific Meeting has grown in many ways, as demonstrated by the increase in attendance and abstract submissions, and diversification of educational offerings, and partners. Most importantly, the scientific rigor and presentation of new knowledge has continued to mature, and the conference is now firmly rooted in the annual educational schedule for medical toxicologists. In anticipation of the upcoming 2025 ASM, we reflect upon the evolution and growth of ACMT’s research and educational agendas, and the Annual Scientific Meeting itself. Keywords: ACMT, Annual Scientific Meeting, Research, Education In April 2025, the American College of Medical Toxicology (ACMT) will hold its Annual Scientific Meeting (ASM) in Vancouver, Canada. The meeting will not only showcase a wide variety of cutting-edge original medical toxicology research, but will also feature a wide range of educational offerings. These include three different concurrent tracts presented in the form of symposia and panel discussions, led by experts in the field. In addition, ASM favorites, such as the Shark Tank Research Forum, Open Mic Competition, and Case-Based Conundrums will remain integral parts of the conference. In anticipation of the upcoming ASM, we took a moment to reflect back upon the evolution and growth of ACMT’s research and educational agendas, and the Annual Scientific Meeting itself. The progenitor of ACMT’s Annual Scientific Meeting was the ACMT Spring Course, first held in April 2002 in Phoenix, Arizona. ACMT’s first stand-alone meeting which was not part of another conference, this two day conference primarily targeted fellows-in-training (FIT). It featured keynote and plenary lectures as well as a research forum where fellows presented early stage research proposals to a panel of senior investigators for constructive feedback. Approximately 60 trainees and members attended this inaugural course. In 2003, a similar FIT targeted course was held in Atlanta, GA. Responding to growing interest from ACMT members for a spring medical toxicology conference that did not just target FIT, a redesigned two-day course was held in March 2006, also in Phoenix, AZ. State of the science medical toxicology lectures were delivered by a national faculty of experts, and attendees included many senior members, recent graduates, and FIT. During these early years, research abstracts were not solicited because ACMT members typically presented their original research at the North American Congress of Clinical Toxicology (NACCT). Over the next several years the ACMT Spring Course became a fixture of the late winter/early spring educational calendar with the meeting regularly held in warm weather locals including Miami, FL in March 2007, San Diego, CA in March 2008, and San Juan, Puerto Rico in March 2009. Each meeting followed a similar 2-day format of keynotes and plenaries, now often programmed with a specific theme such as neurotoxicology or natural toxins. The 2009 Puerto Rico meeting, which was the largest meeting to that date, attracted 112 registrants – about two-thirds ACMT’s full (board-certified) members and the remainder were FIT. In March 2010, the Spring Course was held in Scottsdale, Arizona, and was expanded to three days incorporating professional development sessions, a FIT Research Forum, and the FIT Open Mic Session. These FIT activities provided the opportunity for fellows to present pithy presentations on topics of interest in a friendly competition. With this growth of the Spring Course, members were increasingly asking that an opportunity to present original research be added to the program. In response, a poster session entitled “Research You May Have Missed at Other Meetings” which highlighted original research that was previously presented at an emergency medicine or other conference was added. Over the next 2 years, comparable programs were held in Clearwater, FL in 2011 and San Diego, CA in 2012, which attracted 132 and 164 attendees, respectively. At the 2012 course, for the first time, a pre-conference day-long satellite on a focused topic was offered on the day prior to the start of the general conference session (later known as the ACMT Symposium). The inaugural satellite was titled the “Prescription Opioid Academy” which presented state of the science talks. This symposium was the first of many, covering a wide range of important toxicology topics. At the conclusion of the 2012 conference, the ACMT Board of Directors decided that the inclusion of original research not previously presented at another conference was critical to bringing the best and latest science to the attendees, and for the continued growth of the conference. Starting with the 2013 conference, a call for original abstracts would become part of the planning process and, after adjudication, the accepted abstracts would be presented as either research platforms, or posters, and an abstract compendium would be published in ACMT’s Journal of Medical Toxicology (JMT). This process recruited members of ACMT’s growing Research Committee to assist in review and adjudication of abstracts. In addition, in 2013 the “ACMT Spring Course” was rebranded as the ACMT Annual Scientific Meeting, or ASM. The first ASM was held in San Juan, Puerto Rico in March 2013, and attracted 188 attendees. One hundred and fifteen abstracts were submitted and eighty were accepted for presentation. Over the next 4 years ASM was held in Phoenix, AZ (2014), Clearwater, FL (2015), Huntington Beach, CA (2016), and for the 3rd time in San Juan, Puerto Rico (2017) attracting a growing number of attendees. Until 2018, ACMT’s annual conference had almost always been held in a warm weather or resort venue in March. With the desire to diversify venue options, the 2018 ASM was held in Washington, DC in April, and the number of abstract submissions increased to 225 from the prior record of 160, and 331 attended the conference, also a record. With the growth in attendance came a broader audience of full members and FIT, but an international presence as well. Continuing to break away from the resort venue tradition, the 2019 ASM was held in San Francisco, CA and subsequent conferences were scheduled for New York City in 2020, Portland, OR in 2021 and Austin, TX in 2022. However, COVID-19 intervened, and the New York conference was rapidly transitioned to a virtual conference, and the 2021 and 2022 conferences were also held virtually. While the COVID-19 pandemic interrupted in-person conferences for some time, valuable lessons were learned from the quick transition to virtual learning and the subsequent two years of virtual conferences [ 1 ]. ACMT was able to rapidly and successfully adapt to a virtual format within a matter of hours before the conference. This was in part due to ACMT staff’s experience using virtual learning platforms for webinars. Additionally, ACMT’s staff had grown over the years to accommodate the increasing breadth of the college’s research and educational programs, and services to members. Given the new virtual learning climate precipitated by the pandemic, ACMT invested in improved online learning programs. This not only fostered the success of the subsequent two virtual ASM meetings, but also promoted other successful online learning endeavors, such as the Board Review Course, Total Tox Course, and Seminar in Forensic Toxicology as well as strengthened ACMT’s webinar series (e.g., National Case Conference and Addiction Toxicology Conference). The availability to create quality virtual courses has greatly expanded ACMT’s reach, even as ASM transition back to an in person venue. ASM continued to grow and evolve its programming as it returned to in-person in 2023 in San Diego, CA and then back to Washington, DC in 2024. In addition, ACMT began to offer continuing education credits for non-physician members, such as pharmacists, further expanding its audience. A call for proposals was added to the conference planning and the American Academy of Clinical Toxicology organized its own day-long symposia prior to the ACMT Symposia at ASM, demonstrating growing collaboration between the two organizations Beginning in 2024, multiple concurrent educational tracks were offered in order to diversify the agenda and provide more opportunities for member presentations and engagement. For ASM 2024, 272 abstracts were submitted and 216 were accepted [ 2 ]. Figure 1 demonstrates the trends in abstracts over time, with an overall increase in submissions over time as well as more of a focus on research studies rather than case reports. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Trends in eligible abstracts over time Beyond the research tracks, keynotes, and plenaries, ASM also featured scientific sessions planned by the American Academy of Clinical Toxicology and the American Academy of Addiction Medicine, an international panel with representatives from European Association of Poison Centres and Clinical Toxicologists, the Middle East and North Africa Clinical Toxicology Association, and the Asia Pacific Association of Medical Toxicology, and an address delivered by the ACMT Career Achievement Award winner (formerly the Ellenhorn Award). ASM 2024 set another record with 444 attending from 41 states and 15 countries. More than 100 FIT attended the Washington, DC conference, accounting for 23% of the total attendance. Expanded support for trainees has simultaneously evolved with ASM. In 2024, the Shark Tank Forum was reconfigured to provide a $20,000 grant with support from the Medical Toxicology Foundation (MTF). In addition, in 2024, ACMT also offered a medical student and resident travel award and an international travel award, to help provide fundings for trainees to attend ASM to present their research. This is in addition to long standing ACMT travel awards, such as the Michael P. Spadafora Medical Toxicology Travel Award. This reinforces ACMT’s continued mission to support trainees and more globally promote research in the field of medical toxicology. Over the more than 20 years that have elapsed since the inaugural ACMT conference in 2002, the ACMT ASM has grown in many ways as demonstrated by the increase in attendance and abstract submissions, and diversification of educational offerings, and partners. Most importantly, the scientific rigor and presentation of new knowledge has continued to mature, and the conference is now firmly rooted in the annual educational schedule for medical toxicologists. Enhanced networking, partner development, and reaching out to those who share an interest in medical toxicology but may have not previously attended this conference, are high priorities over the coming years. Compared to other medical disciplines, medical toxicology remains a tiny subspecialty unknown to many physicians and other healthcare providers and organizations. ACMT’s Annual Scientific Meeting has become a vital platform to showcase and grow our specialty, highlight scientific advances, and galvanize our members and collaborators to address these many challenges. Our goal is to continue on this positive trajectory and not only grow the conference, but the specialty as a whole. We hope to see you in Vancouver. Acknowledgements The growth and success of ASM would not be possible without the tireless dedication of ACMT’s staff, members of the Planning Committee, Research Committee, Education Committee, and Abstract Reviewers over the years. To name every individual who helped elevate ASM would exceed the allowable space from our publisher. Please know we appreciate the feedback, effort, and support of all those involved in the evolution of ASM since that very first meeting in 2002. We are especially grateful to the inspirational leadership of recent Program Chairs Diane Calello and Kathy Kopec and all past Chairs for ASM’s success. We extend a special thanks to previous ACMT Staff members Jim Wiggins and Tricia Steffey - their service to ASM’s growth is incalculable. Funding No funding was provided for this study. Declarations Financial Disclosures Dr. Wax (Executive Director) and Ms. Dunavin (Senior Education Program Manager) are employed by ACMT. Dr. Amirshahi currently serves as a member on the ACMT board of directors. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Kopec, K.T., Stolbach, A. Transitioning to Virtual: ACMT’s 2020 Annual Scientific Meeting. J. Med. Toxicol. 2020;16:353–355. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 2. Toce M, Goldfine CE, Mazer-Amirshahi M, Meyn A. Welcome to the 2024 ACMT Annual Scientific Meeting. J Med Toxicol. 2024;20:84–85. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ]',8,'2025-10-15 10:07:06','2025-10-15 10:07:06');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4667,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2829199','Themed section: Imaging in pharmacology.','Davenport AP, Daly C.','2010','themed themed themed maffia hilary carswell strathclyde strathclyde transduce eclectic fluorescently briddon succinct bimolecular complementation compartmentalized homodimerization heterodimerization gonadotrophin tails mcardle gnrhr gonadotrophin chaperones radiolabelled autoradiography radiolabel fluorophores dpcpx fluorophore fluorophore fluorophore utlility heterodimerization methven transmitters exocytosis deferens desipramine varicosity florescent ortolano mco cerebrovasculature mco multiphoton tomographs johnstrom johnstrom baril symporter photomicrograph themed welcomes movie briddon kellam fluorophore baril duque vassaux symporter biotherapy briddon pharmthera henstridge sedgley caunt mcardle gonadotrophin johnstrom barret johnstrom barret etb johnstrom pickard mcardle hislop desensitisation gonadotrophin methven α1b α1a elucidates methven α1a α1d α1d ortolano maffia dever rodolico millington simoni shanks kennard jag briddon holliday bimolecular complementation','Abstract This themed section on Imaging in Pharmacology comprising reviews and original articles arose from two symposia held at the Summer Meeting of the British Pharmacological Society in Edinburgh 8–10 July 2009 on Developments in Receptor Imaging and Imaging and Targeting Inflammation in Stroke and Atherosclerosis. The reports cover a broad spectrum of pharmacological studies, from whole animal imaging to gene expression and emphasize the importance of imaging techniques in pharmacology. The development of each new imaging methodology brings pharmacology closer to the ambitious goal of being able to image (simultaneously) each component part of the G-protein-coupled receptor signalling process. This article is part of a themed section on Imaging in Pharmacology. See the reference list for all papers appearing in this section. Keywords: G-protein coupled receptors, fluorescence imaging, confocal microscopy, positron emission tomography, gene therapy A picture is said to be worth a thousand words but its value increases substantially for pharmacologists with the ability to not only visualize biomolecules but extract quantitative information about their interactions using image analysis, ideally in real time and non-invasively. As a result, imaging has become an important tool in virtually every branch of the life science. Developments in imaging receptors both in vitro and in vivo are rapidly progressing with enhancements to both hardware and software which in turn leads to important new methodologies and applications. This themed section on Imaging in Pharmacology comprising two invited reviews and five original articles arose from two symposia held at the Summer Meeting of the British Pharmacological Society in Edinburgh 8–10 July 2009 on Developments in Receptor Imaging organized by Craig Daly (Glasgow, UK) and Imaging and Targeting Inflammation in Stroke and Atherosclerosis organized by Pasquale Maffia, Paul Coats, Hilary Carswell (Strathclyde Institute of Pharmacy & Biomedical Sciences, University of Strathclyde, Glasgow, UK). G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) transduce an eclectic diversity of signals ranging from single photons of light through to peptides and small proteins. GPCRs, particularly from Family A or Class 1 represent targets for nearly half of currently available medicines and continue to be a major focus for the development of novel imaging agents, particularly fluorescently labelled biomolecules ( Daly and McGrath, 2003 ; Briddon and Hill, 2007 ). Rose (2010 ) provides a succinct overview of applying the emerging technique of bimolecular fluorescence complementation to GPCR signalling particularly measuring interactions with G-proteins and β-arrestin. This technique involves the division of a protein reporter into two non-functioning fragments, each fused to the separate partners under investigation. The association of the target proteins then drives recombination of the reporter fragments, yielding measurable functional activity usually fluorescence excitation/emission characteristics of the parent protein. The technique has the potential to answer key questions as to how GPCRs select downstream signalling partners and how these are compartmentalized. Perhaps the most exciting prospect is studying GPCR homodimerization and heterodimerization. In humans, gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) mediates its actions by GPCRs that uniquely have evolved in mammals to lack C-terminal tails that are usually necessary in other receptors for agonist-induced phosphorylation and arrestin binding, desensitization, internalization and signalling ( McArdle et al. , 2002 ). Finch et al. (2010 ) describe an automated fluorescence strategy to demonstrate, as a consequence of this structural modification, GnRH receptors are poorly expressed on the cell surface, most likely reflecting inefficient exit from the endoplasmic reticulum. Significantly, pharmacological intervention using GnRHR antagonists can increase cell surface GnRH receptor expression. As GnRH mediates control of reproduction by stimulating gonadotrophin secretion from the pituitary, these imaging techniques provide a mechanism for testing the efficacy of pharmacological chaperones to increase cell surface receptor expression to provide the rationale for the potential treatment of infertility. Radiolabelled ligands have been widely used to characterize GPCRs for four decades but with the exception of autoradiography combined with electron microscopy, visualization of receptors is limited to tissues or groups of cells. The great advance in the use of fluorescent labelled ligands is that receptor pharmacology can be visualized and quantified in a single cell. However, unlike many isotopes used to radiolabel ligands, the size of most fluorophores in relation to the ligand may result in alterations to the pharmacology of the parent molecule. Using the adenosine A 1 as a GPCR target, Baker et al. (2010 ) have synthesized an extensive range of fluorescent ligands which have been pharmacologically characterized by inhibition of binding of [ 3 H]DPCPX to expressed receptors. They show that binding is influenced not only by the type of fluorophore but also the length of the associated linker. Intriguingly, in living cells, the physicochemical properties of the fluorophore/linker pairing determine where in the environment of the target receptor the fluorophore is placed and this property may ultimately determine the utlility of a particular compound as a fluorescent probe. Daly et al. (2010 ) have exploited the increase in spatial precision that can be achieved by using confocal imaging with fluorescent labelled ligands to visualize receptors binding four dissimilar fluorescent ligands (α 1 -adrenoceptors, β-adrenoceptors, angiotensin II and cannabinoid receptors) in arteries. Within each cell type within the vessel wall including smooth muscle and endothelial cells, each GPCR examined had a distinctive heterogeneous distribution with limited co-localization with other receptors. Such studies using native tissues are important as many interactions between receptors such as heterodimerization assume that both receptors are expressed in the same cell. While heterodimers may be identified in cell lines expressing comparatively high densities of receptors, it is important to determine whether cells from native tissues are also capable. This approach has recently been exploited in vascular smooth muscle cells ( Methven et al. , 2009a , b ;). Fluorescence imaging is not limited to visualizing receptors but has the potential to quantify the uptake of transmitters. The extracellular concentration of noradrenaline depends on its rate of release by exocytosis balanced by its rate of uptake, which in the peripheral nervous system is the norepinephrine transporter (NET). Parker et al. (2010 ) used confocal microscopy to visualize the accumulation of NET substrate in the mature sympathetic nerve terminals of the mouse isolated vas deferens. They showed fluorescence accumulated linearly in nerve terminals, which could be blocked by inhibitors of NET inhibition (desipramine) and reversed by amphetamine. This technique has the potential to study the physiology of NET regulation and the action of NET inhibitors on mature sympathetic terminals in a single nerve terminal varicosity. A major challenge in imaging is to translate results from florescent probes in vitro to in vivo . Ortolano et al . (2010 ) describe their approach towards imaging fluorescent labelled lymphocytes injected into mice 24 h before undergoing permanent left middle cerebral artery occlusion (MCO), an animal model of stroke. Although brains were subsequently cut into section for imaging, recruitment of labelled lymphocytes in the cerebrovasculature and in the cortex, downstream of occluded middle cerebral artery in adoptive transfer mice, was observed at 24 and 48 h MCO using multiphoton laser scanning microscopy. These results suggest direct observations of the dynamic T-cell behaviour can be made in living brain tissue in real time using this technique as well as ex vivo live imaging of immune response after experimental stroke. Positron emission tomography (PET) is a functional imaging technique that is used to study biological processes in vivo . It is the most sensitive technique available to image and quantify receptor distributions in vivo, particularly with the development of tomographs dedicated to small animals such as rodents for preclinical imaging. It has been used extensively to study major neurotransmitter systems and data obtained in a PET scan can provide information regarding tissue physiology or pathophysiology, as well as pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic information (see e.g. Johnström et al. , 2005a , b ;). PET has increasingly been recognized as a very powerful tool to accelerate development and assessment of existing and novel drugs. Johnström et al. (2010 ) report for the first time the use of PET to dynamically image in vivo within target organs the tissue specific conversion of the biologically inactive precursor [ 18 F]-big endothelin-1 to the mature peptide [ 18 F]-endothelin-1 as binding to endothelin receptors. A major site for conversion was within the vasculature of the lung and liver, whereas uptake in kidney was more complex reflecting excretion of [ 18 F]-Big ET-1 without conversion to ET-1. Finally, in the new era of regenerative medicine, Baril et al. (2010 ) review strategies for non-invasive imaging of gene therapy, to allow the precise spatiotemporal measurement of gene expression particularly in longitudinal studies involving gene transfer vectors. For the imaging technique to be effective, it should provide precise information about the location, magnitude and persistence of a specific transgene over time. The authors focus on the sodium iodide symporter (NIS), describing the biological function of the system to transport molecules across a phospholipid membrane using an electrochemical gradient of sodium ions (Na+) as carrier and an adenosine triphosphate-driven pump. The imaging potential of NIS to monitor gene expression in the live subject using nuclear imaging technology is considered including trafficking of stem cells. Many pharmacological studies result in the generation of an image of some sort such as a blot, gel or photomicrograph. However, as alluded to above, a number is worth a thousand pictures and it is important to extract as much quantitative information as possible from our images. With the developments of live cell imaging and ultra fast scanning, it is now possible to visualize biological events in real time. The reports published in this ‘imaging’ themed section cover a broad spectrum of pharmacological studies, from whole animal imaging to gene expression. These reports emphasize the importance of imaging techniques in pharmacology. Furthermore, the development of each new fluorescent probe and imaging methodology brings us ever closer to the goal of being able to image (simultaneously) each component part of the GPCR signalling process. British Journal of Pharmacology welcomes manuscripts employing imaging technologies to study pharmacological processes and is developing ways to support authors, such as online supplementary data that can be in movie or 3D display formats. Note that colour in figures is completely free of cost to the author. References Baker JG, Middleton R, Adams L, May LT, Briddon SJ, Kellam B, et al. Influence of fluorophore and linker composition on the pharmacology of fluorescent adenosine A1 receptor ligands. Br J Pharmacol. 2010;159:772–786. doi: 10.1111/j.1476-5381.2009.00488.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Baril P, Martin-Duque P, Vassaux G. Visualization of gene expression in the live subject using the Na/I symporter as a reporter gene: applications in biotherapy. Br J Pharmacol. 2010;159:761–771. doi: 10.1111/j.1476-5381.2009.00412.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Briddon SJ, Hill SJ. Pharmacology under the microscope: the use of fluorescence correlation spectroscopy to determine the properties of ligand-receptor complexes. Trends Pharmacol Sci. 2007;28:637–645. doi: 10.1016/j.tips.2007.09.008. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Daly CJ, McGrath JC. Fluorescent ligands, antibodies, and proteins for the study of receptors. Pharmacol Ther. 2003;100:101–118. doi: 10.1016/j.pharmthera.2003.08.001. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Daly CJ, Ross RA, Whyte J, Henstridge CM, Irving AJ, McGrath JC. Fluorescent ligand binding reveals heterogeneous distribution of adrenoceptors and ‘cannabinoid-like’ receptors in small arteries. Br J Pharmacol. 2010;159:787–796. doi: 10.1111/j.1476-5381.2009.00608.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Finch AR, Sedgley KR, Armstrong SP, Caunt CJ, McArdle CA. Trafficking and signalling of gonadotrophin-releasing hormone receptors: an automated imaging approach. Br J Pharmacol. 2010;159:751–760. doi: 10.1111/j.1476-5381.2009.00413.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Johnström P, Fryer TD, Richards HK, Barret O, Davenport AP. Dynamic in vivo imaging of receptors in small animals using positron emission tomography. Methods Mol Biol. 2005b;306:217–232. doi: 10.1385/1-59259-927-3:217. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Johnström P, Fryer TD, Richards HK, Harris NG, Barret O, Clark JC, et al. Positron emission tomography using 18F-labelled endothelin-1 reveals prevention of binding to cardiac receptors owing to tissue-specific clearance by ETB receptors in vivo. Br J Pharmacol. 2005a;144:115–122. doi: 10.1038/sj.bjp.0706064. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Johnström P, Fryer TD, Richards HK, Maguire JJ, Clark JC, Pickard JD, et al. Positron emission tomography of [18F]-big endothelin-1 reveals renal excretion but tissue specific conversion to [18F]- endothelin-1 in lung and liver. Br J Pharmacol. 2010;159:812–819. doi: 10.1111/j.1476-5381.2010.00641.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] McArdle CA, Franklin J, Green L, Hislop JN. Signalling, cycling and desensitisation of gonadotrophin-releasing hormone receptors. J Endocrinol. 2002;173:1–11. doi: 10.1677/joe.0.1730001. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Methven L, McBride M, Wallace GA, McGrath JC. The α1B/d-adrenoceptor knockout mouse permits isolation of the vascular α1A-adrenoceptor and elucidates its relationship to the other subtypes. Br J Pharmacol. 2009b;158:209–224. doi: 10.1111/j.1476-5381.2009.00269.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Methven L, Simpson PC, McGrath JC. α1A/B-Knockout mice explain the native α1D-adrenoceptor''s role in vasoconstriction and show that its location is independent of the other α1D-subtypes. Br J Pharmacol. 2009a;158:1663–1675. doi: 10.1111/j.1476-5381.2009.00462.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ortolano F, Maffia P, Dever G, Rodolico G, Millington OR, De Simoni MG, et al. Advances in imaging of new targets for pharmacological intervention in stroke: real-time tracking of T-cells in the ischaemic brain. Br J Pharmacol. 2010;159:808–811. doi: 10.1111/j.1476-5381.2009.00527.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Parker LK, Shanks JA, Kennard JAG, Brain KL. Dynamic monitoring of NET activity in mature murine sympathetic terminals using a fluorescent substrate. Br J Pharmacol. 2010;159:797–807. doi: 10.1111/j.1476-5381.2009.00574.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Rose RH, Briddon SJ, Holliday N. Bimolecular fluorescence complementation: lighting up seven transmembrane domain receptor signalling networks. Br J Pharmacol. 2010;159:738–750. doi: 10.1111/j.1476-5381.2009.00480.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',39,'2025-10-15 10:07:10','2025-10-15 10:07:10');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4668,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11706350','The selection of target areas for orbital imaging, application of diethylenetriaminepentaacetic acid orbital imaging, clinical factors, alkaline phosphatase, and thyrotropin receptor antibodies in the staging and grading of thyroid-associated ophthalmopathy.','Yuan M, Feng Y, Guo L, Li P, Liu Y, Wang Y, Chen Y, Jin G.','2024','ophthalmopathy nospecs exophthalmos thyrotropin nospecs nonactive diethylenetriaminepentaacetic ophthalmopathy ophthalmopathy ophthalmopathy proptosis disfigurement orbitopathy eugogo i131 i131 diethylenetriaminepentaacetic ujhelyi galuska diffuses distributes photophobia tearing tearing bellies thyrotropin triiodothyronine ft3 harbin nospecs eugogo orbitopathy nospecs eugogo orbitopathy harbin yjsky2023 precedence 99mo jiangyuan wuxi jiangyuan wuxi radiochemical ft3 orbits coregistration rectus rectus rectus rectus rectus rectus rectus rectus ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean proptosis ft3 proptosis nospecs nonnormally ft3 nospecs thyrotropin p25 p75 diethylenetriaminepentaacetic protrusion protrusion ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean rectus rectus rectus rectus enlargements diethylenetriaminepentaacetic retrobulbar nonnormally nonnormally exophthalmos exophthalmos ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean exophthalmos exophthalmos ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean nospecs nospecs a318 nospecs nospecs nospecs nospecs nospecs ft3 exophthalmos exophthalmos ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean ft3 triiodothyronine thyrotropin ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean ur2max ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean thyrotropin ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean ur2max ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean thyrotropin ur2max ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean ur2max ur2max ur2max nonactive nonactive methimazole methimazole nonactive a319 tearing nonactive nontarget r1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean ur2max ur1max ur1mean ur2max ur2mean nospecs tsi tsi extrathyroidal ur2max tsi nospecs nonactive ur2max ur2max ur2max harbin nuclearmedicinecomm doddapaneni blessing bimatoprost orbitopathy ophthalmopathy riguetto tambascia zantut wittmann bartalena kahaly baldeschi dayan eckstein marcocci eugogo orbitopathy eugogo orbitopathy g43 g67 bhatti dutton neuroophthalmol zaat derikx zwaveling soonawala trotsenburg mooij sabini ionni rocchi vitti marcocci orbitopathy ujhelyi erdei galuska szabados balazs retrobulbar diethylenetriamine pentaacetic ophthalmopathy galuska leovey szucs farkas szabados garai berta orbitopathy depreotide rinderknecht krauthammer taplin uszler effros solutes selter gire sikder ophthalmopathy kizilca oztek kesimal senol rwj ezra dayan orbitopathy yorke braverman bartalena piantanida tanda orbitopathy ophthalmopathy bioeng cockerham padnick stuertz sedlak robledo ophthalmopathy bucci giuliani masetti moshkelgosha kohling covelli banga berchner pfannschmidt indigo orbitopathy','Abstract Purpose This study aimed to investigate the active phase of thyroid-associated ophthalmopathy (TAO), its correlations with clinical factors, serological tests, and orbital imaging parameters, and evaluate the diagnostic value of different orbital imaging target areas. Methods A total of 45 patients with thyroid-related eye disease underwent imaging, serological tests, and clinical data collection. Clinical Activity Score (CAS) assessment, diplopia scores, and NOSPECS grading were conducted. Radioactive counts of extraocular muscles and tear glands were measured. Computed tomography scans assessed exophthalmos and extraocular muscle thickening. Correlations and differences among study parameters and grades were evaluated using receiver operating characteristic curves. Results Active TAO correlated significantly with radioactive counts of muscles and tear glands, alkaline phosphatase (ALP), thyrotropin receptor antibody (TRAb), and age. Significant differences were found among NOSPECS grades for studied variables (except ALP and TRAb). Extraocular muscle thickening was confirmed as a reliable diagnostic criterion. High consistency was found between orbital imaging and CAS staging. Treatment showed varying degrees of improvement in active patients, while nonactive patients showed no progression during follow-up. Receiver operating characteristic curves demonstrated high diagnostic efficacy for tear gland radioactive counts. Conclusion Tear glands and extraocular muscles have high diagnostic value in TAO, with tear glands showing a higher value. Orbital imaging provides an objective and comprehensive assessment compared with CAS scoring alone. ALP, TRAb, and age also play significant roles in staging. Keywords: alkaline phosphatase, diethylenetriaminepentaacetic acid, single-photon emission computed tomography/computed tomography, target area selection, thyroid-associated ophthalmopathy, thyroid globulin antibodies Introduction Thyroid-associated ophthalmopathy (TAO), also known as Graves’ ophthalmopathy (GO), is an organ-specific autoimmune disease that can lead to inflammation of the extraocular muscles, orbital fat, and connective tissue [ 1 ]. It ranks first in the incidence of adult orbital diseases [ 2 ]. This condition not only causes changes in appearance such as proptosis, lid retraction, lid lag, and eye movement disorders but also often accompanies symptoms such as eye pain, dryness, foreign body sensation, diplopia, and decreased visual acuity. Additionally, it carries potential risks of disability and disfigurement [ 3 ]. The course of TAO can be divided into active and inactive phases, and clinical evaluation is commonly performed using the Clinical Activity Score (CAS) [ 4 ] from the European Group on Graves’ Orbitopathy (EUGOGO) guidelines. Treatment approaches vary significantly for patients in different stages. For those in the active phase, hormone therapy, immunosuppressive agents, and drug treatment are commonly employed. As time progresses and the eye disease enters the inactive phase, the efficacy of conventional medical interventions, however, diminishes. In such cases, orbital decompression surgery may be required for treatment [ 5 ]. In addition, radioactive iodine (I131) therapy is one of the effective methods for treating hyperthyroidism [ 6 ]. If a patient simultaneously suffers from TAO and the eye disease is in the active phase, using I131 therapy for hyperthyroidism treatment, however, may potentially exacerbate ocular symptoms [ 7 ]. Therefore, accurately staging TAO plays a crucial role in guiding treatment. 99m Tc-diethylenetriaminepentaacetic acid (DTPA) is currently primarily used for assessing kidney function, but some studies have indicated that DTPA can be applied in ocular imaging. Research by Ujhelyi et al . [ 8 ] shows that this imaging method is safe, not only for staging but also for predicting the effectiveness of corticosteroid treatment. Galuska et al . [ 9 ] confirmed that DTPA may be comparable to octreotide in identifying inflammation in GO, and it is significantly more cost-effective. Therefore, we chose the radiotracer 99m Tc-DTPA for ocular imaging. The mechanism is as follows: 99m Tc-DTPA is a sensitive marker for inflammatory activity due to its hydrophilic properties and negative charge, which prevent it from entering cells. Instead, it diffuses through the capillary walls and distributes in the extracellular fluid, where it is subsequently cleared from circulation by the kidneys. In TAO patients, the permeability of capillaries in the inflamed orbital region is increased, allowing it to ‘leak’ from the vascular bed into the interstitial fluid and bind with peptides present in the extracellular fluid at the site of inflammation, thus reflecting the degree of inflammatory activity in the TAO orbit [ 10 ]. The strength of its correlation with CAS, however, varies in previous studies. Additionally, there is scarce research on the selection of target areas for staging TAO diagnosis. TAO patients often present with lacrimal gland involvement, manifesting as symptoms of aqueous-deficient dry eye such as foreign body sensation, photophobia, wind sensitivity, and tearing [ 11 ]. Moreover, the extraocular muscles are commonly affected in TAO, with 75–80% of thyroid eye disease cases involving these muscles [ 12 ]. When TAO patients are in the active inflammatory phase, they may exhibit congestion, swelling, and inflammatory cell infiltration in both the lacrimal gland and extraocular muscles. 99m Tc-DTPA serves as a sensitive marker for inflammatory activity. Furthermore, it is well-known that conjunctivitis (also known as pink eye) can affect the lacrimal gland and extraocular muscles, leading to symptoms such as red eyes, swollen eyelids, tearing, and discharge. These symptoms are also observed in TAO (also known as GO). TAO, however, is characterized by computed tomography (CT) findings of thickened muscle bellies without thickening of the muscle tendons. Therefore, in 99m Tc-DTPA single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT)/CT orbital imaging, patients in the active phase of the disease are expected to show higher radionuclide uptake in the lacrimal gland and extraocular muscles compared to those in the inactive phase, and this imaging technique can be utilized to differentiate conjunctivitis from TAO. In this study, a quantitative approach was employed, with the extraocular muscles and lacrimal gland selected as target regions, to compare the value of related parameters in assessing the inflammatory activity of TAO. Some scholars have suggested that the pathogenesis of TAO may be related to mechanisms such as increased expression of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) receptors in fibroblasts or connective tissue cells in orbital fat and extraocular muscle tissue, leading to the production of corresponding autoantibodies in the body. This condition is often associated with abnormalities in thyrotropin receptor antibody (TRAb) [ 13 ]. Previous studies have shown that 15–79% of hyperthyroidism patients have varying degrees of liver dysfunction, which is related to the abnormal metabolism caused by elevated thyroid hormones and autoimmune damage to the liver [ 14 ]. Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) is a significant indicator of liver function, and its levels in hyperthyroidism patients are often higher than those in healthy individuals. Additionally, 25–50% of hyperthyroidism patients develop TAO [ 15 ], and TAO is most commonly seen in hyperthyroidism patients [ 4 ]. Currently, there is scarce research on the correlation between TAO staging, grading, and liver function indicator ALP. The data from this study are worthy of further accumulation. Moreover, previous studies on TRAb have mainly focused on TAO staging assessment, and data related to TRAb and grading are lacking. Therefore, this study analyzes the impact factors of TAO patients’ orbital imaging results, clinical characteristics, and relevant serum indicators such as TRAb, ALP, free triiodothyronine (FT3), free thyroxine (FT4), and TSH. Receiver-operating characteristic (ROC) curves are established for significant data to assess their diagnostic efficacy, aiming to provide clinical reference. Materials and methods General information Clinical data were collected from 45 patients with TAO who underwent 99m Tc-DTPA SPECT/CT orbital fusion imaging at the Department of Nuclear Medicine, Second Affiliated Hospital of Harbin Medical University, between November 2023 and June 2024. Patients then underwent clinical assessments, including CAS evaluation, diplopia scoring, and grading according to the American NOSPECS classification, at the ophthalmology department 1 day after imaging. The collected clinical information included patients’ names, genders, ages, thyroid functional status, duration of thyroid dysfunction, smoking status, and other relevant data. Inclusion criteria Patients meeting the diagnostic criteria for TAO according to the 2021 EUGOGO Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Management of Grave’s Orbitopathy [ 16 ]. Age ≥ 16 years. Patients who have not received any treatment for eye disease or liver-related conditions in the past 3 months. Exclusion criteria Patients with eye diseases other than mild refractive errors. Patients with autoimmune diseases. Patients with liver function impairment caused by conditions other than thyroid dysfunction. Pregnant or lactating women. According to the CAS, TAO patients were divided into an active phase group (CAS ≥ 3, n = 27) and an inactive phase group (CAS < 3, n = 18). According to the American NOSPECS classification in the 2021 EUGOGO Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Management of Graves’ Orbitopathy, TAO patients were graded from 0 to 6. All patients undergoing examinations provided informed consent, and the research protocol was approved by the Medical Ethics Committee of the Second Affiliated Hospital of Harbin Medical University (Ethics Approval No: YJSKY2023-453). Main drugs and equipment The SPECT/CT imaging system used is the Precedence model manufactured by Philips, Amsterdam, Netherlands. The 99Mo-99mTc generator is produced by Beijing Atom High-Tech Co., Ltd, Beijing, China. The radioactive drug 99m Tc-DTPA for injection is provided by Jiangyuan Pharmaceutical Factory, Wuxi Jiangyuan Industrial Technology and Trade Co., Ltd., (Wuxi, Jiangsu Province, China) with a radiochemical purity of >95% for 99m Tc-DTPA. Serum levels of ALP, TRAb, FT3, FT4, and TSH were measured by relevant departments in our hospital following standard operating procedures. Single-photon emission computed tomography/computed tomography image analysis After intravenous injection of 99m Tc-DTPA ranging from 555 MBq (15 mCi) to 740 MBq (20 mCi), SPECT/CT imaging of the orbits was performed 20 min later. The SPECT and CT images were fused using the built-in workstation software to achieve coregistration of the images. Considering that the amount of injected radiotracer may affect the counts of radioactive uptake, this study used ratios with respect to the background to minimize this interference. In the transverse section images, the image slice with the highest radioactive uptake in the lacrimal gland and extraocular muscle regions, along with the adjacent two slices, was selected. The bilateral lacrimal glands and the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and lateral rectus muscles were delineated as regions of interest (ROIs). The ROI at the level of the optic nerve in the occipital bone was selected as the background, and the ratio of radioactive tracer uptake between the lacrimal gland and the optic nerve (UR lacrimal gland), superior rectus and optic nerve (UR superior), inferior rectus and optic nerve (UR inferior), medial rectus and optic nerve (UR medial), and lateral rectus and optic nerve (UR lateral) was calculated. Additionally, the maximum counts of radioactive uptake in the extraocular muscles on both sides were averaged, and the ratio of this average to the average counts of radioactive uptake in the occipital bone was calculated as T1/TN max (UR1max). Similarly, the average counts of radioactive uptake in the extraocular muscles on both sides were averaged, and the ratio of this average to the average counts of radioactive uptake in the occipital bone was calculated as T1/TN mean (UR1mean). Furthermore, the maximum and average counts of radioactive uptake in the lacrimal glands on both sides were averaged, and the ratio of this average to the average counts of radioactive uptake in the occipital bone was calculated as T2/TN max (UR2max) and T2/TN mean (UR2mean), respectively. By normalizing the pixels in the selected ROI using this method, accurate counting ratios can be obtained. Image acquisition was performed using a tube voltage of 140 kV, tube current of 100 mA, and a matrix size of 128 × 128, with a 20-min acquisition time. After image acquisition, physicians from the Department of Nuclear Medicine delineated the ROIs and determined the staging (without knowledge of other examination results to prevent bias). The degree of proptosis was measured for each patient, and the presence of extraocular muscle thickening was assessed, with the number of affected extraocular muscles scored from 0 to 4 for each patient. Acquisition of serological results All study participants underwent fasting blood collection of 3–4 ml in vacuum coagulation tubes on the morning of the SPECT/CT examination. The blood samples were analyzed by personnel from the respective departments for ALP, TRAb, FT3, FT4, and TSH, and the corresponding data were provided. Acquisition of ophthalmic results All study participants visited the ophthalmology department for assessment on the second day following the SPECT/CT examination. A designated ophthalmologist evaluated the patients’ ocular status to exclude other causes of proptosis and performed a CAS assessment, diplopia scoring, and American NOSPECS classification. Statistical analysis The statistical analysis was conducted using SPSS 27.0 software [International Business Machines Corporation (IBM), Armonk, New York, USA]. For normally distributed variables, the independent samples t test was used for comparison, while for nonnormally distributed variables, the Mann–Whitney U test was applied. The chi-square test was used for comparing categorical data. The Kruskal–Wallis H test was employed to compare differences among groups with different levels. Results Baseline characteristics After reviewing the inclusion and exclusion criteria, basic data analysis was conducted for the 45 patients (Table 1 ). Table 1. Baseline characteristics Variables Value Clinical variables Age (years) 42.29 ± 11.27 a Sex (male) 16 (35.6%) b Thyroid function status (hyperthyroidism) 35 (77.8%) b Abnormal thyroid function time 24.00 (6.00–60.00) c Smoking 21 (46.7%) b Laboratory variables FT3 4.49 (3.54,5.48) c FT4 12.46 ± 6.49 a TSH 0.43 (0.02–4.22) c ALP 99.00 (66.00–129.00) c TRAb 4.00 (1.58–15.58) c Ophthalmic evaluation results CAS score (≥3) 27 (60.0%) b Diplopia score (3) 4 (8.9%) b NOSPECS classification (0) 3 (6.7%) b Open in a new tab ALP, alkaline phosphatase; CAS, Clinical Activity Score; TRAb, thyrotropin receptor antibody; TSH, thyroid-stimulating hormone. a Mean ± SD. b Frequency (percentage). c Median (P25–P75). Analysis of 99m Tc-diethylenetriaminepentaacetic acid orbital single-photon emission computed tomography/computed tomography imaging parameters The radiological findings indicated that 28 patients were in the active phase (62.2%), while 17 patients were in the inactive phase (37.8%). The median left eye protrusion was 20.40 mm (interquartile range: 18.85–21.60), and the median right eye protrusion was 20.30 mm (interquartile range: 19.20–22.25). The median UR1max was 1.91 (interquartile range: 1.61–2.36), and the median UR1mean was 1.53 (interquartile range: 1.31–2.14). The median UR2max was 2.03 (interquartile range: 1.65–2.56), and the median UR2mean was 1.80 (interquartile range: 1.42–2.17). Among the patients, 42 showed varying degrees of thickening of the eye muscles. Specifically, 32 patients exhibited thickening of the inferior rectus and medial rectus muscles. Additionally, 16 patients had thickening of the superior rectus muscle, while 10 patients had thickening of the lateral rectus muscle. The distribution of patients based on the number of eye muscle enlargements (0–4) was as follows: 3 patients, 14 patients, 12 patients, 11 patients, and 5 patients. Analysis of 99m Tc-diethylenetriaminepentaacetic acid orbital single-photon emission computed tomography/computed tomography images The imaging results revealed that in patients with active TAO, there was a significant increase in the uptake of radioactive isotopes in the lacrimal gland, extraocular muscles, and retrobulbar fat tissues, manifesting as the characteristic ‘Monkey’s eye sign’. In contrast, patients with inactive TAO showed either a mild increase or no increase in radioactive isotope uptake (Fig. 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Images of active and inactive patients. (a) The uptake of radionuclides in lacrimal glands and extraocular muscles was significantly increased in the active phase. The position shown by the yellow arrow is typical of its symbol: ‘Monkey eye sign’. (b) Image of a patient in the inactive phase showing minimal to no increase in uptake of radioactive isotopes in the lacrimal gland and extraocular muscles. To compare the difference among different stages Independent samples t tests were conducted using CAS score and eye imaging results as grouping variables to analyze age and FT4. For CAS score, significant differences were found between the two groups in age ( P = 0.015), but not in FT4 levels ( P = 0.701). Similarly, for eye imaging results, significant differences were observed in age ( P = 0.037), while FT4 levels did not show significant differences ( P = 0.480). The Mann–Whitney U test was used with CAS score as the grouping variable to analyze nonnormally distributed quantitative data, and the results are shown in Table 2 . Similarly, the Mann–Whitney U test was conducted with SPECT/CT imaging results as the grouping variable to analyze nonnormally distributed quantitative data, and the results are presented in Table 3 . Table 2. Clinical Activity Score results comparison of active and inactive parameters Variables Total Active phase ( n = 27) Inactive phase ( n = 18) P value Exophthalmos (left) 20.40 (18.85–21.60) 20.20 (18.90–21.00) 20.50 (19.08–22.60) 0.660 Exophthalmos (right) 20.30 (19.20–22.25) 20.50 (19.20–22.00) 20.60 (18.85–22.63) 0.772 Enlarged extraocular muscles (number of people) 42 24 18 0.157 UR1max 1.91 (1.61–2.36) 2.20 (1.43–2.19) 1.64 (1.49–1.83) <0.001 UR1mean 1.53 (1.31–2.14) 1.79 (1.61–2.36) 1.36 (1.23–1.42) 0.002 UR2max 2.03 (1.65–2.56) 2.36 (2.03–2.60) 1.67 (1.48–1.96) <0.001 UR2mean 1.80 (1.42–2.17) 2.08 (1.77–2.27) 1.44 (1.31–1.70) <0.001 Open in a new tab Table 3. Single-photon emission computed tomography/computed tomography results of active and inactive parameters comparison Variables Total Active phase ( n = 28) Inactive phase ( n = 17) P value Exophthalmos (left) 20.40 (18.85–21.60) 20.50 (18.98–21.35) 20.30 (18.85–22.30) 0.797 Exophthalmos (right) 20.30 (19.20–22.25) 20.55 (19.20–22.15) 20.30 (18.50–22.55) 0.897 Enlarged extraocular muscles (number of people) 42 26 16 0.157 UR1max 1.91 (1.61–2.36) 2.21 (1.86–2.58) 1.62 (1.48–1.80) <0.001 UR1mean 1.53 (1.31–2.14) 1.79 (1.46–2.24) 1.35 (1.22–1.39) <0.001 UR2max 2.03 (1.65–2.56) 2.39 (2.04–2.62) 1.67 (1.48–1.90) <0.001 UR2mean 1.80 (1.42–2.17) 2.10 (1.79–2.28) 1.44 (1.29–1.65) <0.001 Open in a new tab Using count data, chi-square tests were conducted with CAS score results and SPECT/CT imaging results as grouping variables, respectively. The results indicated no significant differences ( P > 0.05) between the two groups for gender, smoking status, thyroid function status, muscle hypertrophy count, and diplopia score. To compare the difference among different grading groups Kruskal–Wallis H tests were performed on various quantitative variables categorized by grade. The pie chart and bar graph showing the number of patients in each grade of NOSPECS classification are depicted in Fig. 2 . The box plots of quantitative data among different NOSPECS grades are shown in Supplementary Figure 1, Supplemental digital content 1, http://links.lww.com/NMC/A318 . The chi-square test was used to compare count data across different grades, with the results detailed in Table 4 . Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Pie chart and bar graph depicting the number of patients in each grade of NOSPECS classification. (a) A pie chart depicting the number of patients in each grade of NOSPECS classification, with proportions for grades 0–6 represented by light blue, dark green, purple, light pink, green, and dark blue colors, respectively. (b) A bar graph of the number of patients in each grade of NOSPECS classification. Table 4. Chi-square test of count data between different NOSPECS classes Variables P value CAS results <0.001 Nuclear medicine imaging report <0.001 Sex 0.808 Smoking 0.180 Thyroid function status (hyperthyroidism) 0.427 Enlarged extraocular muscles (number of people) 0.126 Diplopia score <0.001 Open in a new tab CAS, Clinical Activity Score. Correlation analysis between patient characteristics and Clinical Activity Score The Spearman rank correlation analysis was conducted to assess the relationship between patient characteristics and CAS scores. The results are shown in Table 5 . Table 5. Rank correlation analysis with Clinical Activity Score Variables Correlation coefficient P value Nuclear medicine imaging report 0.766 <0.001 NOSPECS classification 0.734 <0.001 Age (years) 0.396 0.007 Sex (male) −0.152 0.320 Thyroid function status (hyperthyroidism) −0.181 0.234 Abnormal thyroid function time −0.091 0.553 Smoking 0.071 0.643 FT3 0.005 0.976 FT4 0.082 0.592 TSH −0.054 0.724 ALP 0.442 0.002 TRAb 0.514 <0.001 Diplopia score 0.464 0.001 Enlarged extraocular muscles (number of people) 0.310 0.038 Exophthalmos (left) −0.003 0.983 Exophthalmos (right) 0.068 0.656 UR1max 0.710 <0.001 UR1mean 0.653 <0.001 UR2max 0.734 <0.001 UR2mean 0.723 <0.001 Open in a new tab ALP, alkaline phosphatase; FT3, free triiodothyronine; FT4, free thyroxine; TRAb, thyrotropin receptor antibody; TSH, thyroid-stimulating hormone. Concordance of Clinical Activity Score and single-photon emission computed tomography/computed tomography diagnosis by kappa test The results of the kappa test, comparing the CAS scores determining whether patients were in the active phase with the interpretations from nuclear medicine physicians based on SPECT/CT, showed a kappa value of 0.860, with a significance level of P < 0.001. This indicates a strong consistency between the CAS scores and the SPECT/CT interpretations. The kappa test of consistency between CAS scoring and SPECT/CT diagnosis is shown in Fig. 3 . Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Kappa test plot of concordance between CAS score and SPECT/CT diagnosis, where ‘0’ represents CAS results indicating the inactive phase, and ‘1’ represents CAS results indicating the active phase. In SPECT/CT imaging results, blue denotes the inactive phase, and green denotes the active phase. CAS, Clinical Activity Score; SPECT/CT, single-photon emission computed tomography/computed tomography. Receiver-operating characteristic curve analysis Based on the CAS results, the ROC curve analysis showed that the area under the curve (AUC) values for age ( r = 0.389, sensitivity = 55.6%, specificity = 83.3%), ALP ( r = 0.463, sensitivity = 85.2%, specificity = 61.1%), TRAb ( r = 0.537, sensitivity = 70.4%, specificity = 83.3%), UR1max ( r = 0.574, sensitivity = 74.1%, specificity = 83.3%), UR1mean ( r = 0.574, sensitivity = 74.1%, specificity = 83.3%), UR2max ( r = 0.704, sensitivity = 81.5%, specificity = 88.9%), and UR2mean ( r = 0.630, sensitivity = 74.1%, specificity = 88.9%) were 0.704, 0.709, 0.757, 0.796, 0.770, 0.835, and 0.825, respectively. These results indicate that UR2max exhibited the best diagnostic performance. The ROC curve based on CAS results is shown in Fig. 4 . Fig. 4. Open in a new tab ROC curve based on CAS results. In the ROC curve, the light blue, dark green, purple, orange, brown, light green, and dark blue curves, respectively, represent age, ALP, TRAb, UR1max, UR1mean, UR2max, and UR2mean. ALP, alkaline phosphatase; CAS, Clinical Activity Score; ROC, receiver-operating characteristic; TRAb, thyrotropin receptor antibody. Similarly, based on the interpretations from imaging results, the ROC curve analysis showed that the AUC values for age ( r = 0.360, sensitivity = 54.0%, specificity = 82.4%), ALP ( r = 0.504, sensitivity = 86.0%, specificity = 64.7%), TRAb ( r = 0.561, sensitivity = 68.0%, specificity = 88.2%), UR1max ( r = 0.632, sensitivity = 75.0%, specificity = 88.2%), UR1mean ( r = 0.632, sensitivity = 75.0%, specificity = 88.2%), UR2max ( r = 0.762, sensitivity = 82.0%, specificity = 94.1%), and UR2mean ( r = 0.691, sensitivity = 75.0%, specificity = 94.1%) were 0.674, 0.732, 0.791, 0.855, 0.819, 0.893, and 0.880, respectively. Again, UR2max exhibited the best diagnostic performance. The ROC curve based on film reading is shown in Fig. 5 . Fig. 5. Open in a new tab ROC curve based on the results of film reading. In the ROC curve, the light blue, dark green, purple, orange, brown, light green, and dark blue curves, respectively, represent age, ALP, TRAb, UR1max, UR1mean, UR2max, and UR2mean. ALP, alkaline phosphatase; ROC, receiver-operating characteristic; TRAb, thyrotropin receptor antibody. Our serological markers ALP and TRAb also demonstrated some diagnostic efficacy, although not as excellent as UR2max. Due to the superior diagnostic performance of TRAb and parameters related to the ROI, we subsequently conducted a correlation analysis between UR1max, UR1mean, UR2max, UR2mean, and TRAb. The results indicated that only UR2max was correlated with TRAb ( P = 0.028). Multiple regression analysis factors affecting UR2max We used UR2max as the dependent variable and other continuous variables as factors for analysis. The results indicated that only TSH and ALP were significant, but their correlation was weak (R2 = 0.357, adjusted R2 = 0.235). Cases with inconsistent clinical activity scores and single-photon emission computed tomography/computed tomography results Among the 45 patients included in the study, there were three cases where discrepancies were observed in the results. Patients 17 and 19 were classified as nonactive phase based on CAS results, while orbital examination indicated an active phase. Patient 16 was classified as active phase based on CAS results, whereas SPECT/CT examination indicated a nonactive phase. We suggest that patients classified as active phase based on orbital imaging results receive relevant treatment. For those with mild symptoms, medication intervention is recommended, while patients with moderate to severe symptoms may require steroid therapy, and surgery may be necessary if deemed appropriate. The curative effect observed after treatment Three patients underwent a second examination after treatment. Patient 19 had a dosage adjustment of oral methimazole tablets, increasing from 0.75 tablet/day to 1 tablet/day, and was re-examined after 41 days. Patient 38 received four periorbital injections of triamcinolone and simultaneous adjustments of oral Methimazole tablet dosage, reduced from 1 tablet/day to 0.5 tablet/day, as well as adjustments of oral levothyroxine dosage, reduced from 0.25 tablet/day to 0.125 tablet/day, and was re-examined after 75 days. Patient 45 received four periorbital injections of triamcinolone and oral prednisone therapy for 1.5 months and was re-examined after 44 days. The results showed varying degrees of improvement in all three patients, confirming the accuracy of the initial assessment. Additionally, we recommend treatment for Patient 17, with a follow-up examination scheduled after 2 months. Since the initial examination for Patient 17 was on 16 May and the scheduled follow-up has not yet occurred, there, however, are no corresponding results available. For Patient 16, whose SPECT/CT examination indicated a nonactive phase, no intervention was performed, but the patient was followed up for 3 months, during which no progression of eye disease was observed. The images before and after the treatment of the patient are shown in Supplementary Figure 2–4, Supplemental digital content 2, http://links.lww.com/NMC/A319 . Discussion TAO is an organ-specific autoimmune disease associated with thyroid dysfunction [ 17 ]. It is most commonly seen in patients with hyperthyroidism but can also occur in those with hypothyroidism or normal thyroid function. The clinical manifestations of this disease are diverse, ranging from light sensitivity, tearing, diplopia, and strabismus to potential optic nerve involvement leading to blindness [ 18 ]. Additionally, some patients may develop psychological conditions such as depression and anxiety as a result of TAO [ 19 ]. Even after the acute phase, patients may continue to experience impaired vision, changes in appearance, and psychosocial effects [ 19 ]. Although various disciplines in clinical practice have methods to determine the staging of patients with TAO, SPECT/CT, due to its advantage of combining anatomy and metabolism, along with the favorable cost-effectiveness of the imaging agent DTPA used in this study, presents strong competitiveness. Our imaging results demonstrate that in active-phase TAO patients, there is a significant increase in radioactive uptake in orbital tissues such as the lacrimal gland, extraocular muscles, and retro-orbital fat, with the characteristic feature being the ‘monkey eye sign’. In contrast, nonactive-phase TAO patients show mild or no increase in radioactive uptake. Given that TAO patients often have involvement of extraocular muscles and lacrimal gland tissue, we delineated the four extraocular muscles and lacrimal gland tissue as ROIs in this study. Considering the potential impact of injection dose differences on uptake counts in the ROI and potential biases in radioactive counting of occipital brain tissue due to its complex structure, we selected the occipital cranial bone at the level of the optic nerve as the background (nontarget area) for delineation. This operation was performed by a fixed nuclear medicine physician to eliminate the influence of individual habits. We selected R1max, UR1mean, UR2max, and UR2mean as the study targets, and the results showed that lacrimal gland parameters had better diagnostic efficacy in determining the stage of eye disease, especially UR2max. Additionally, the extraocular muscles also exhibited high diagnostic efficacy, although they may be influenced by radioactive uptake in the nasopharyngeal area, leading to decreased accuracy. Although efforts were made to avoid the nasopharyngeal area when delineating ROIs, some influence may still exist. Furthermore, the diagnostic efficacy of the mean radioactive uptake of extraocular muscles and lacrimal gland tissue was lower than that of the maximum value, possibly due to unaffected areas of the extraocular muscles or small regions of the lacrimal gland tissue. Nevertheless, these two indicators still have strong diagnostic significance. Similarly, there are significant differences in UR1max, UR1mean, UR2max, and UR2mean among different NOSPECS grades. Multiple studies have shown a significant correlation between levels of thyroid stimulating immunoglobulin (TSI) and the occurrence and severity of TAO. Due to the complexity and time-consuming nature of its detection process, TSI testing, however, is currently limited to specialized laboratories and not widely used in clinical practice [ 20 ]. Nevertheless, TRAb is more common and can be tested in some primary hospitals. Previous studies have indicated its crucial pathogenic role in determining the extrathyroidal manifestations of hyperthyroidism and Graves’ disease such as eye disease [ 21 ]. Therefore, it can be considered as an alternative to assist in the diagnosis of TAO. ALP levels are often elevated in patients with hyperthyroidism and can serve as an auxiliary indicator for clinical diagnosis. Whether it has value in staging thyroid-related eye diseases, however, has rarely been explored, so we included this indicator in the study. Our research results demonstrate that these two serological indicators have a certain value in determining the stage and grade, providing some reference significance for clinical practice. Therefore, accurate staging and grading of patients, along with early intervention, are crucial to minimizing physical and psychological harm. Given the diverse clinical presentations of TAO, some patients may have active disease despite subtle findings on the CAS. Thus, finding more objective diagnostic methods and identifying relevant clinical and laboratory indicators is paramount. Although various methods exist in clinical practice to stage patients with TAO, SPECT/CT stands out due to its advantageous combination of anatomical and metabolic features, along with the cost-effectiveness of the imaging agent DTPA used in our study. Given the involvement of extraocular muscles and lacrimal gland tissue in TAO patients, we delineated these structures as ROIs. The occipital cranial bone at the level of the optic nerve served as the background for delineation to mitigate the potential influence of differences in injected radioactive doses and biases in radioactive counting. We found that lacrimal gland parameters exhibited better diagnostic efficacy in determining the stage of eye disease, especially UR2max. Additionally, extraocular muscles also showed high diagnostic efficacy, although they may be influenced by radioactive uptake in the nasopharyngeal area. Although TSI testing is complex and time-consuming, TRAb testing is more accessible and may serve as an alternative to assist in the diagnosis of TAO. Serum ALP levels, often elevated in hyperthyroidism, may also have diagnostic value in staging thyroid-related eye diseases. Subsequent Spearman rank correlation analysis revealed significant correlations between patient data and CAS scores, with the US NOSPECS classification showing the strongest correlation. Consistency testing between CAS scores and SPECT/CT diagnoses yielded a high kappa value, indicating strong agreement. There, however, were three cases of inconsistent diagnoses, suggesting the potential difficulty in diagnosis at the border between active and nonactive phases. Treatment based on SPECT/CT imaging results proved effective for these patients, indicating its value in staging TAO. Further analysis showed differences in indicators between stages using CAS and imaging results as standards, with ROC curve analysis demonstrating their diagnostic value. Factors related to lacrimal gland > extraocular muscles > TRAb > ALP > age in terms of diagnostic efficacy. TRAb is correlated with UR2max. Multivariate linear regression analysis indicated that ALP and TSH influenced UR2max values. Unfortunately, our study was limited by its single-center design and small sample size. Nevertheless, it provides valuable insights for clinical practice. Conclusion We conducted orbital imaging on patients with TAO, revealing varying degrees of radiotracer uptake across different stages of the disease. The delineated ROIs showed significant diagnostic value, with UR2max exhibiting the highest diagnostic efficacy. Additionally, TRAb and ALP also held diagnostic value for staging TAO and could assist clinicians in staging. Orbital imaging, compared to ophthalmic CAS scoring, is a more objective and comprehensive examination method, playing a crucial role in the diagnosis, staging, treatment selection, prognosis prediction, and efficacy observation of TAO patients. Acknowledgements Supported by Harbin Medical University Horizontal Project (Project Code: 070500020293). M.Y. and G.J. proposed the idea of the article; M.Y., Y.F., L.G., Y.L., and Y.W. performed the literature search; M.Y. and Y.C. wrote the article; and Y.F., P.L., G.J., and L.G. submitted the article and replied to the letter; all authors have no objections. Conflicts of interest There are no conflicts of interest. Supplementary Material nmc-46-152-s001.pdf (127.4KB, pdf) nmc-46-152-s002.pdf (256.7KB, pdf) Footnotes Supplemental Digital Content is available for this article. Direct URL citations appear in the printed text and are provided in the HTML and PDF versions of this article on the journal’s website, www.nuclearmedicinecomm.com . References 1. Choi CJ, Tao W, Doddapaneni R, Acosta-Torres Z, Blessing NW, Lee BW, et al. The effect of prostaglandin analogue bimatoprost on thyroid-associated orbitopathy. Invest Ophthalmol Vis Sci. 2018; 59:5912–5923. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Huang Y, Fang S, Li D, Zhou H, Li B, Fan X. The involvement of T cell pathogenesis in thyroid-associated ophthalmopathy. Eye (Lond). 2019; 33:176–182. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Riguetto CM, Neto AM, Tambascia MA, Zantut-Wittmann DE. The relationship between quality of life, cognition, and thyroid status in Graves’ disease. Endocrine. 2019; 63:87–93. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Bartalena L, Kahaly GJ, Baldeschi L, Dayan CM, Eckstein A, Marcocci C, et al.; EUGOGO. The 2021 European Group on Graves’ orbitopathy (EUGOGO) clinical practice guidelines for the medical management of Graves’ orbitopathy. Eur J Endocrinol. 2021; 185:G43–G67. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Bhatti MT, Dutton JJ. Thyroid eye disease: therapy in the active phase. J Neuroophthalmol. 2014; 34:186–197. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Zaat AS, Derikx JPM, Zwaveling-Soonawala N, van Trotsenburg ASP, Mooij CF. Thyroidectomy in pediatric patients with Graves’ disease: a systematic review of postoperative morbidity. Eur Thyroid J. 2021; 10:39–51. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Sabini E, Ionni I, Rocchi R, Vitti P, Marcocci C, Marinò M. Occurrence of Graves’ orbitopathy and Graves’ hyperthyroidism after a trauma to the eye. Eur Thyroid J. 2018; 7:51–54. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Ujhelyi B, Erdei A, Galuska L, Varga J, Szabados L, Balazs E, et al. Retrobulbar 99mTc-diethylenetriamine-pentaacetic-acid uptake may predict the effectiveness of immunosuppressive therapy in Graves’ ophthalmopathy. Thyroid. 2009; 19:375–380. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Galuska L, Leovey A, Szucs-Farkas Z, Szabados L, Garai I, Berta A, et al. Imaging of disease activity in Graves’ orbitopathy with different methods: comparison of (99m)Tc-DTPA and (99m)Tc-depreotide single photon emission tomography, magnetic resonance imaging and Clinical Activity Scores. Nucl Med Commun. 2005; 26:407–