INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4685,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6371374','Detecting Technical Image Quality in Radiology Reports.','Mabotuwana T, Bhandarkar VS, Hall CS, Gunn ML.','2018','commoditization hedge generalizations hedging punctuation processor parses subsegmental streak hardening overexposure underexposure streak hardening streak hardening suspend streak obscures streak hardening mammo underexposure mrn xrs xas brat annotating brat brat runoff negated streak streak hardening dictation nonsensical dictation macros parse abujudeh kaewlai asfaw thrall towbin pryor srinivasa kasales succinct mahgerefteh yam blachar sosna mccoubrie lindley gillies hassell sender mansfield fool perpetuated rosanowsky wah rejects e112 e117 krupinski andriole walz flannigan magnuson schueler armbrust owusu banahene darko hasford asirifi roohi shalemaei ncr306 polman brat brat nlplab powerbi minn zandieh filice notifying strahan jolley dictation motyer liddy torreggiani dictation s11845 golnari forsberg rosipko','Abstract Image interpretation accuracy is critical to ensure optimal care, yet many diagnostic reports contain expressions of uncertainty often due to shortcomings in technical quality among other factors. While radiologists will usually attempt to interpret images and render a diagnosis even if the imaging quality is suboptimal, often the details related to any quality concerns are dictated into the report. Despite imaging exam quality being an import factor for accurate image interpretation, there is a significant knowledge gap in terms of understanding the nature and frequency of technical limitations mentioned in radiology reports. To address some of these limitations, in this research we developed algorithms to automatically detect a broad spectrum of acquisition-related quality concerns using a dataset containing 1,210,858 exams. There was some type of a quality concern mentioned in 2.4% of exams with motion being the most frequent. Introduction Improving the value of imaging, defined as quality per unit cost, is an issue of critical importance in radiology. Measuring and improving image quality is essential not only to ensure optimal diagnostic accuracy and compliance with regulatory requirements, but also for imaging centers to be competitive in an environment where there is a growing trend toward commoditization of certain radiology services. Quality in radiology is multi-dimensional and includes measuring, monitoring and improving various aspects of the workflow, including examination appropriateness, procedure protocol, accuracy of interpretation, timely communication of imaging results as well as performance improvements in safety and efficiency [ 1 ]. Radiology-specific key performance indicators have been developed across multiple strategic areas to promote best practices that can lead to improved outcomes in patient care [ 1 , 2 ] and various other quality initiatives have been introduced to improve the consistency of radiology reports, primarily through the use of standardized structured reporting [ 3 ]. Diagnostic accuracy is critical to ensure optimal patient care, and while it is sometimes possible to be definitive in radiological diagnoses, in most cases, radiological interpretation is heavily influenced by the clinical circumstances of the patient, clinical context, previous imaging, various biases radiologists may not be aware of and many other factors. This makes image interpretation a non-binary process (normal vs abnormal, cancer vs “all-clear”) [ 4 ], yet it is important that ambiguous “hedge” vocabulary, undefined modifiers, double negatives, and generalizations be avoided when possible [ 5 ]. Although there are various guidelines and hospital policies (e.g., peer-review [ 6 , 7 ]) encouraging radiologists to avoid hedging and strive to improve communication with referring clinicians [ 8 ], around 35% of diagnostic reports are reported to contain expressions of uncertainty [ 9 ]. Lack of diagnostic confidence can be due to a number of factors, including shortcomings in the acquisition device, acquisition parameters, protocol selection, inadequate image processing, contrast administration, anatomic variations, patient body habitus, and artifacts [ 10 ]. In fact, in the daily radiology practice, the rate of interpretation error is reported to be between 3% and 4% [ 11 ]; however, of the radiology studies that contain abnormalities, the error rate is much higher, averaging in the 30% range [ 12 ]. Most of prior research has focused on identifying image rejection rates [ 13 , 14 ], which usually is a case of very substandard image quality. Although various recommendations have been made to improve quality of acquired images [ 15 ] and minimize artifacts [ 16 , 17 ], image rejection rates remain high, ranging from 11-17% for radiography depending on the hospital setting [ 13 , 18 - 20 ]. While radiologists will usually attempt to interpret images and render a diagnosis even if the exam is suboptimal, often the details related to suboptimal exam are dictated into the report. Despite suboptimal image quality being an import factor for accurate image interpretation, there is a significant knowledge gap in terms of understanding what type of quality concerns are explicitly mentioned in the radiology reports as well as their frequency. To address some of these limitations, in this research we developed algorithms to automatically detect a broad spectrum of quality issues from radiology reports, primarily as related to acquisition related concerns. Methods Dataset We extracted data for radiology exams performed between January 1 2012 and December 31 2014 from the University of Washington radiology information system. Dataset contained 1,219,876 exams performed across multiple network hospitals. For each exam, the dataset contained the report text as well as several meta-data fields, including exam code, exam date, subspecialty, patient setting (inpatient, outpatient or emergency) and modality. This study was approved as a quality improvement project and IRB-exempt. The distribution of exams by widely used modalities was: radiography (XR): 622,367 (51.0%), computed tomography (CT): 229,058 (18.8%), ultrasound (US): 136,177 (11.2%), magnetic resonance imaging (MR): 89,298 (7.3%), mammography (MG): 66,329 (5.4%), nuclear medicine (NM): 22,344 (1.8%), fluoroscopy (FL): 21,051 (1.7%), interventional (XA): 18,118 (1.5%) and positron emission tomography (PT): 6,116 (0.5%). Remaining 9,018 exams (accounting for less than 1%) were performed outside of the network hospitals and/or were for modalities with low volume, and therefore excluded from analysis. Report processing pipeline Radiology reports typically contain several sections. Although the sections are not standardized across all institutions, the reports typically contain sections equivalent to Clinical Information to represent patient’s presenting signs, symptoms and reason for examination, Technique to include the scanning technique used, Comparison to mention a relevant prior studies that were used for comparison, Findings to describe imaging observations made by the radiologist, and the Impression/Conclusion section to summarize the radiologists’ diagnostic interpretation of their observations. Findings and Impression sections could be combined for short reports, and sometimes there would be an Addendum section as well which includes information added after a finalized report, for a wide variety of reasons. For the work presented herein, we focus on the findings, impression and addendum sections only because they usually contain any quality concerns relevant to current exam images. An exemplary radiology report containing multiple quality concerns is shown in Figure 1 . Figure 1. Open in a new tab A radiology report with a reference to a motion artifact. Underlining is added for emphasis and is not present in the original report. All reports were first pre-processed to identify the individual report sections for known variations in section titles (e.g., some reports may contain Summary instead of Impression), spacing, and punctuation. The pre-processor then parses the sections to extract individual sentences within each section. The quality concern extraction algorithm is then attempts to determine quality concerns, if any, at the sentence level. Algorithm Development Based on the experience of a senior clinical radiologist (MG), we identified a broad set of routinely encountered technical image quality concerns mentioned in radiology reports. An initial attempt was made to create a list of all concerns and detect these concerns from report text, but after several study design related discussions, the authors decided that a ‘tag-based’ approach would be more suitable since this provides the flexibility to associate parts of the report text with multiple quality concerns. For instance, the text “Limited evaluation for subsegmental pulmonary embolism due to contrast bolus timing and respiratory motion artifact” can be associated with “Contrast opacification” as well as “Motion”. The types of high level quality concerns that were identified were: Motion Positioning Streak / hardening (CT only) Susceptibility (MR only) Contrast opacification Patient / study identification error Patient size / body habitus Incomplete study obstetrics (US only) Incorrect exposure (XR only) Noise Mammogram suboptimal Suboptimal NOS Artifact NOS For each of the quality concern types above, we created a list of commonly used expressions and phrases (based on domain knowledge) that are routinely used to describe the type of concern. For some of the high-level concern types described above, we also included several sub-categories to provide more granularity. For instance, “Contrast” was created as a sub-category of “Contrast opacification” to flag concerns related to contrast enhancement, while “Overexposure” and “Underexposure” were created as sub-categories of “Incorrect exposure”. We then developed algorithms, primarily using regular expressions, to identify occurrences of quality concerns in the radiology reports. All regular expression matches were performed at the sentence level. Table 1 shows several example phrases that suggest the presence of at least one type of quality concern. A given phrase can be associated with up to five concern types (although this limit can be extended as needed). Table 1: Example phrases suggestive of at least one type of quality concern Phrase Concern Type (1) Concern Type (2) Concern Type (3) streak artifact; artifact from embolization coils (and other hardware); beam hardening artifact Streak / hardening Suboptimal due to patient body habitus, rapid breathing and inability to suspend breathing Patient size / habitus Motion There is motion and streak artifact that obscures portions of the right coronary artery Streak / hardening Motion Poor opacification, motion artifact, and poor SNR Contrast opacification Motion Noise For technical reasons (positioning), repeat craniocaudal images are recommended Mammo suboptimal Positioning Study is limited due to underexposure and body habitus Exposure Patient size / habitus wrong patient; wrong mrn; Identification error Incomplete anatomic survey due to advanced gestational age Incomplete study obstetrics Open in a new tab We developed algorithms using a training dataset containing 500 exams which was created using keyword searches (due to low prevalence) to randomly sample the larger dataset until all concern types were sufficiently represented (a minimum of 25 exams was required for each category). There were 482 true positives and 18 false positives resulting in 96.4% accuracy (95% CI: 96.4-96.4%). There were no false-negatives and by definition, no true-negatives due to selection of the validation set. Algorithm was then improved until 100% accuracy was achieved. Algorithm validation To validate our algorithm’s ability to correctly identify quality concerns mentioned in radiology reports, we created a validation set containing 100 exams that contained at least one quality concern as per algorithm. To create this dataset, we ran the algorithm over the full dataset and then sampled the dataset for 100 exams based approximately on the modality distribution of the dataset (exams that were part of training were excluded). This resulted in the test dataset having 35 XRs, 23 CTs, 23 MRs, 9 USs, 2 PTs, 2 MGs, 2 NMs, 2 RFs and 2 XAs exams. Similarly, we also created a dataset containing 100 exams with the same distribution that did not contain any quality concerns per algorithm. These exams were needed in order to identify any cases that the algorithm may have missed, if any (i.e., false-negatives). A brat annotation server [ 21 ] was setup and the radiology reports were loaded so that a radiologist annotator could perform validation. Where applicable, reports were pre-annotated to highlight the quality concern(s) the algorithm detected in order to minimize the overhead associated with annotating 200 reports. Figure 2 shows a sample report in the brat annotation environment. Figure 2: Open in a new tab Pre-annotated report in brat environment Author VB, a Board Eligible Radiologist, performed validation of the 200 exams. If the pre-annotations were acceptable no changes were made, otherwise, the annotations were edited as needed (i.e., incorrect annotations were deleted, and any missing annotations were added). There were 92 true-positives, 8 false-positives, 1 false-negative and 99 true-negatives, resulting in accuracy of 95.5% (95% CI: 91.4-96.4%). Evaluation was based on the algorithm’s ability to correctly identify a sentence as having a valid quality concern as well as capturing the exhaustive list of categories. For instance, for sentence containing “poor opacification, motion artifact, poor SNR” to be considered a true-positive, the algorithm output had to be the three tags: “contrast opacification”, “motion” and “noise”. Similarly, sentence “The graft there is poor opacification/incomplete filling of three-vessel runoff of the left lower leg.” was associated with “Contrast opacification” category by the algorithm and this was considered a false-positive by the radiologist because it referred to a diagnostic finding rather than an image quality concern, although ‘poor opacification’ appears in the sentence. The only false-negative was due to sentence “The lateral view is inadequate for evaluation of joint effusion” which was associated with “Positioning” category by VB. False-positives were mainly in relation to having some form of reference to a prior exam, or a negated finding (e.g., “not well seen on prior scan due to streak artifact”, “No areas of focal susceptibility artifact are seen to suggest pericardial calcifications”). These errors were all subsequently corrected using rules (except for the false-negative which requires taking exam context into consideration) and the algorithm was re-run on the full dataset. Results There were 29,467 (2.4%) reports that contained at least one type of quality concern. Table 2 shows the distribution of quality concerns by imaging modality and Table 3 shows the distribution by type of concern (note that an exam can be associated with multiple concern types). Table 2: Exams with quality concerns by scanned modality Modality #Exams #Exams with at least one quality concern %of Exams with at least one quality concern MR 89,298 5,618 6.3% CT 229,058 13,969 6.1% PT 6,116 362 5.9% US 136,177 5,611 4.1% FL 21,051 560 2.7% XA 18,118 354 2.0% NM 22,344 328 1.5% MG 66,329 281 0.4% CR 622,367 2,384 0.4% Total 1,210,858 29,467 2.4% Open in a new tab Table 3: Number of exams with quality concerns, by type of concern Concern Category #Exams %of Exams with quality concerns (n = 29,467) %of All Exams (n = 1,210,858) Motion 9,307 31.6% 0.8% Contrast opacification 8,640 29.3% 0.7% Streak / hardening (CT only) 4,546 15.4% 0.4% Patient size / body habitus 3,236 11.0% 0.3% Susceptibility (MR only) 1,776 6.0% 0.1% Incomplete study obstetrics (US only) 1,217 4.1% 0.1% Suboptimal NOS 939 3.2% 0.1% Artifact NOS 671 2.3% 0.1% Noise 598 2.0% <0.1% Mammogram suboptimal 230 0.8% <0.1% Patient / study identification error 83 0.3% <0.1% Positioning 52 0.2% <0.1% Incorrect exposure (XR only) 38 0.1% <0.1% Total 31,333 Open in a new tab In order to provide radiology administrators with a quick overview of departmental trends and the ability to monitor the effectiveness of any quality improvement interventions over time, we also developed an interactive dashboard ( Figure 3 ) using Microsoft Power BI [ 22 ] that shows the number of reports containing quality concerns by modality, subspecialty, exam description as well as by year. Various filters are provided so that a user can explore to better understand the data and identify potential opportunities for improvement. The tool allows the user to easily explore the underlying data that contributes towards a particular metric (commonly referred to as a “drill down” capability), for instance, a user can click on the Motion bar showing ‘9.3k’ to examine the underlying report that contain motion related quality concerns. Figure 4 shows another view of the dashboard where the user can explore the data by scanning resource. Figure 3: Open in a new tab Dashboard showing quality concerns Figure 4: Open in a new tab shows another view of the dashboard where the user can explore the data by scanning resource. Discussion In this manuscript, we outlined a generic report processing pipeline that can be used to determine shortcomings in technical image quality using radiology reports, with a focus on detecting acquisition related errors. We also developed an interactive dashboard to enable department administrators to identify potential root causes and monitor effectiveness of any quality improvement interventions. Using a production dataset containing over a million exams, we demonstrated how radiology reports can be used to identify potential acquisition-related quality concerns. A key strength of this work is the integration of multiple components to provide an end-to-end solution, starting with a raw data extract from the radiology information system all the way through to automatically updating a dashboard that can potentially be used to support quality improvement initiatives. Our results suggest that prevalence of quality concerns is substantial, especially for magnetic resonance imaging, computed tomography and positron emission tomography. We have neither attempted to quantify the severity of the radiologists’ image quality concerns in each report nor their diagnostic impact. Hence, how these shortcomings actually affect the diagnostic capabilities is largely unknown and needs further investigation. To the best of the authors’ knowledge, this is the first study where efforts have been made to identify technical image quality concerns from radiology reports, although prior research has explored ways to identify dictation related errors such as wrong-word substitution, nonsensical phrases, wrong laterality and gender-specific discrepancies [ 23 - 25 ], which can lead to unnecessary confusion, possible harm to patient and reduce the credibility of the radiologists. Despite using a production dataset containing data from multiple network hospitals, the current study has several limitations. First, all reports were created using common dictation macros that are shared across the network hospitals and therefore the methods used to parse radiology reports may not be readily generalizable to other institutions. Second, the algorithm has limitations when evaluating the clinical context of an exam, as observed by the false-negative we encountered. As a result, it is possible that our numbers may slightly under-represent the true prevalence. As such, some further refinement and validation of the algorithm are necessary. Third, we have not evaluated the true impact of quality concerns in this study. Not all concerns may have an adverse impact on the patient, yet it is important to ‘close the loop’ to determine the ultimate impact of acquisition-related quality concerns on actual diagnostic accuracy, and subsequent patient care. The generic processing pipeline we have developed can be used to identify a range of technical image quality concerns routinely mentioned in radiology reports. We found a significantly higher rate of quality issues than has been previously reported (0.25%) [ 26 ]. In this prior study, radiologists were required to submit cases for review when they identified them during reporting. Having the means to automatically detect quality concerns contained in radiology reports without the requirement for radiologists to make the additional effort to submit the case could more accurately reflect the rate of technical image quality shortcomings and provide valuable insights to radiology quality improvement initiatives. While radiology reports usually mention only the most severe cases of technical image quality concerns, it is anticipated by addressing the causes of these issues that overall imaging quality might improve. Proactively identifying and addressing acquisition errors in imaging studies may help radiology departments improve clinical value. References 1. Rubin D.L. Radiographics. Informatics in radiology: Measuring and improving quality in radiology: meeting the challenge with informatics. 2011;31(6):1511–27. doi: 10.1148/rg.316105207. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Abujudeh H.H, R Kaewlai, Asfaw B.A, Thrall J.H. Radiographics. Quality initiatives: Key performance indicators for measuring and improving radiology department performance. 2010;30(3):571–80. doi: 10.1148/rg.303095761. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Larson D.B, A.J Towbin, R.M Pryor, L.F Donnelly. Radiology. Improving consistency in radiology reporting through the use of department-wide standardized structured reporting. 2013;267(1):240–50. doi: 10.1148/radiol.12121502. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Brady A.P. Insights Imaging. Error and discrepancy in radiology: inevitable or avoidable? 2017;8(1):171–182. doi: 10.1007/s13244-016-0534-1. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Srinivasa Babu A, M.L Brooks. Radiographics. The malpractice liability of radiology reports: minimizing the risk. 2015;35(2):547–54. doi: 10.1148/rg.352140046. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Yang C, C.J Kasales, T Ouyang, et al. SAGE Open Med. A succinct rating scale for radiology report quality. 2014;2:2050312114563101. doi: 10.1177/2050312114563101. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Mahgerefteh S, Kruskal J.B, Yam C.S, Blachar A, Sosna J. Radiographics. Peer review in diagnostic radiology: current state and a vision for the future. 2009;29(5):1221–31. doi: 10.1148/rg.295095086. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Wallis A, P McCoubrie. Clin Radiol. The radiology report—are we getting the message across? 2011;66(11):1015–22. doi: 10.1016/j.crad.2011.05.013. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Lindley S.W, Gillies E.M, Hassell L.A. Pathol Res Pract. Communicating diagnostic uncertainty in surgical pathology reports: disparities between sender and receiver. 2014;210(10):628–33. doi: 10.1016/j.prp.2014.04.006. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Reiner B. J Digit Imaging. Uncovering and improving upon the inherent deficiencies of radiology reporting through data mining. 2010;23(2):109–18. doi: 10.1007/s10278-010-9279-4. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Kim Y.W, L.T Mansfield. AJR Am J Roentgenol. Fool me twice: delayed diagnoses in radiology with emphasis on perpetuated errors. 2014;202(3):465–70. doi: 10.2214/AJR.13.11493. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Berlin L. AJR Am J Roentgenol. Accuracy of diagnostic procedures: has it improved over the past five decades? 2007;188(5):1173–8. doi: 10.2214/AJR.06.1270. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Hofmann B, T.B Rosanowsky, C Jensen, K.H Wah. Acta Radiol Open. Image rejects in general direct digital radiography. 2015;4(10):2058460115604339. doi: 10.1177/2058460115604339. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Mount J. Radiography. Reject analysis: A comparison of radiographer and radiologist perceptions of image quality. 2016;22(2):e112–e117. [ Google Scholar ] 15. Krupinski E.A, Williams M.B, Andriole K, et al. J Am Coll Radiol. Digital radiography image quality: image processing and display. 2007;4(6):389–400. doi: 10.1016/j.jacr.2007.02.001. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Walz-Flannigan A, Magnuson D, Erickson D, Schueler B. AJR Am J Roentgenol. Artifacts in digital radiography. 2012;198(1):156–61. doi: 10.2214/AJR.11.7237. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Jimenez D.A, Armbrust L.J, O’Brien R.T, Biller D.S. Vet Radiol Ultrasound. Artifacts in digital radiography. 2008;49(4):321–32. doi: 10.1111/j.1740-8261.2008.00374.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Owusu-Banahene J, Darko E.O, Hasford F, Addison E.K, Asirifi J.O. Journal of Radiation Research and Applied Sciences. Film reject analysis and image quality in diagnostic Radiology Department of a Teaching hospital in Ghana. 2014;7(4):589–594. [ Google Scholar ] 19. Roohi Shalemaei R. Radiat Prot Dosimetry. Films reject analysis for conventional radiography in Iranian main hospitals. 2011;147(1-2):220–2. doi: 10.1093/rpd/ncr306. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Jones A.K, Polman R, Willis C.E, Shepard S.J. J Digit Imaging. One year’s results from a server-based system for performing reject analysis and exposure analysis in computed radiography. 2011;24(2):243–55. doi: 10.1007/s10278-009-9236-2. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. brat rapid annotation tool. [cited 2018 Feb 26]; Available from http://brat.nlplab.org/index.html . 22. Microsoft. Power BI. [cited 2018 Mar 10]; Available from https://powerbi.microsoft.com . 23. Minn M.J, Zandieh A.R, Filice R.W. J Digit Imaging. Improving Radiology Report Quality by Rapidly Notifying Radiologist of Report Errors. 2015;28(4):492–8. doi: 10.1007/s10278-015-9781-9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Chang C.A, Strahan R, Jolley D. J Digit Imaging. Non-clinical errors using voice recognition dictation software for radiology reports: a retrospective audit. 2011;24(4):724–8. doi: 10.1007/s10278-010-9344-z. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Motyer R.E, Liddy S, Torreggiani W.C, Buckley O. Ir J Med Sci. Frequency and analysis of non-clinical errors made in radiology reports using the National Integrated Medical Imaging System voice recognition dictation software. 2016;185(4):921–927. doi: 10.1007/s11845-016-1507-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Golnari P, Forsberg D, Rosipko B, Sunshine J.L. J Digit Imaging. Online Error Reporting for Managing Quality Control Within Radiology. 2016;29(3):301–8. doi: 10.1007/s10278-015-9820-6. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',14,'2025-10-15 10:08:08','2025-10-15 10:08:08');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4686,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6140179','Comparison of High-Fidelity Medical Simulation to Short-Answer Written Examination in the Assessment of Emergency Medicine Residents in Medical Toxicology.','Christian MR, Sergel MJ, Mycyk MB, Aks SE.','2017','mcgahie evokes guessing clabsi proctoring salicylate nacct steff errick clockwise msma truman sergel mycyk stroger tmcmed 0b013e3181cea265 domont intv utterback sportsman nasca philibert nejmsr1200117 mcgahie 0b013e3181ffacdb bong lightdale fredette 0b013e3181e98b29 0b013e3181c82aaa okuda bryson demaria msj potts muret wagstaff','Abstract We compared high-fidelity medical simulation to short-answer written examination in the assessment of emergency medicine residents (EMR) on a month-long medical toxicology rotation. Knowledge-based assessment tools using cases of an aspirin overdose and a tricyclic antidepressant overdose were used to assess all consecutive rotating EMR (n=53). Assessment by simulation had similar accuracy and precision but higher satisfaction rates when compared to written examination. Incorporating simulation into the ABEM certifying examination warrants further study. Introduction Medical simulation is a popular and accepted teaching tool for post-graduate education. In addition, simulation is increasingly being used as an assessment tool particularly for procedures. 1 , 2 , 3 However, data are limited regarding the use of simulation as a tool for competency assessment. Some have advocated for the use of simulation in board certification and re-certification exams. 3 , 4 , 5 Furthermore, simulation may have several advantages over traditional written tests in assessing procedural competency and certain American College of Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) core competencies (interpersonal & communication skills, professionalism, patient care, and systems-based practice). The next GME system, which started in July 2013, incorporates “milestones” into the traditional core competencies. 6 Since the types of procedures and complexity of simulation cases can be increased or decreased without much difficulty depending on the learner’s level of training, simulation may lend itself to assessing these “milestones” more easily than traditional written tests for physicians-in-training. In a similar vein, a study by McGahie et al. demonstrated that standard examination tools like the USMLE Steps 1 and 2 are not correlated with reliable measures of medical students’, residents’, and fellows’ clinical skill acquisition. Simulation may be a more accurate assessment tool, but more research is needed. 7 Also, simulation evokes a physiologic response in physicians comparable to real-life clinical scenarios. 8 Despite these possible advantages, medical simulation is much more expensive to proctor than written exams, and comparative research is warranted 9 , 10 . We sought to assess precision and accuracy of sim-based testing versus traditional written-based testing. Methods Knowledge-based assessment tools using sim and written examinations were developed, piloted, and revised by four physicians (authors) board-certified in Medical Toxicology and/or Emergency Medicine. These physicians were familiar with the Emergency Medicine core content requirements set forth by the American Board of Emergency Medicine (ABEM). From July 2011 to June 2012, all consecutive rotating EMR on a month-long Medical Toxicology rotation at an academic Emergency Medicine training program were eligible for participation in the study. Ten board-certified medical toxicologists as well as four fellows in training staffed the Medical Toxicology rotation. The rotation consists of daily patient rounds, daily didactics, and a weekly journal club. At any given time, approximately 20–25 medical students, pharmacy students, pediatric residents, emergency medicine residents, and pediatric emergency medicine fellows are on the service. However, only emergency medicine residents were assessed in this pilot study. On odd months, EMR were assessed using a sim case of an aspirin (ASA) overdose patient and a written examination of a tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose patient. On even months, testing modalities were switched (TCA sim and ASA written). The assessment tools each contained ten knowledge-based questions. For each toxin (ASA and TCA), the scenario, questions, and answers were exactly the same; only the assessment tool (sim versus written) varied. We specifically avoided the use of multiple-choice questions as an assessment modality as this would give a resident at least a 20% chance of guessing the correct answer (if five answer choices were given). Instead, we chose to compare two open-ended assessment modalities (sim versus written). At the end of the assessment, the EMR completed a survey. The content of the survey included demographic items, previous experience with simulation, and learner satisfaction. EMR were blinded to the purpose of the study. Scores were not used in the final rotation grade. Final scores were reported as 0 to 10 based upon the number of knowledge-based questions answered correctly (out of 10). The fourth author scored both the simulation and written tests to ensure consistency in grading. Data were analyzed using descriptive and inferential statistics. This study received IRB approval. Results From July 2011 to June 2012, 53 EMR [23 females, 30 males; PGY 3(38), 4(11), and 5(4)] from six different emergency medicine residencies were eligible for our study and all participated. All EMR were considered senior residents by their home institution. All residents who participated completed the study. Simple, descriptive statistics were used. Examination number, mean scores, and standard deviations were: ASA sim: N=26, M=6.04 SD=2.38; ASA written: N=27, M=7.30 SD=1.27; TCA sim: N=27, M=6.41 SD=1.22; and TCA written: N=26, M=6.27 SD=2.20 (see Figure 1 ). Although our study was not powered to find a correlation between sim and written examinations, there was no statistically significant Spearman correlation between ASA sim and written (ρ=0.698, p=0.742) or between TCA sim and written (ρ=−0.222, p=0.276). Both the written tests (51/53; 96%) and sim tests (53/53) were judged to be fair (as opposed to not fair) by EMR. On a three-point Likert scale (very satisfied, satisfied, and not satisfied), the sim tests had a higher satisfaction rate (45/53 very satisfied; 85%) than the written tests (27/53 very satisfied; 51%) (see Figure 2 ). Although only 6/53 EMR (11%) had been assessed using simulation previously, the majority of EMR (38/53; 72%) would prefer to see simulation incorporated into the American Board of Emergency Medicine (ABEM) certifying (oral) examination. Open in a new tab Open in a new tab Discussion We feel that our study has several important implications. In regards to graduate medical education, written examinations have many shortcomings in the assessment of future physicians. Specifically, it is very difficult, if not impossible, to assess some of the ACGME’s core competencies (i.e., interpersonal & communication skills, professionalism, patient care, and systems-based practice) with a written examination. Medical simulation may be a better tool to assess some of these areas. In addition, medical simulation has a distinct advantage regarding patient safety in comparison to other methods of assessment like direct observation. The era of “see one, do one, teach one” is quickly ending in the wake of patient safety. In fact, some literature demonstrates that simulation-based education can have significant impact on patient outcomes (i.e., lowering the central line-associated bloodstream infection (CLABSI) rate at two participating hospitals). 11 Furthermore, some literature supports the use of simulation-based assessment tools in the assessment of residents in an Anesthesiology residency. 12 However, to our knowledge, no such data exist regarding residents training in Emergency Medicine and/or Medical Toxicology. While medical simulation is certainly more expensive than proctoring a standard written test, it may be more cost effective than other commonly used methods of assessment like the so-called “standardized patient.” These actor-patients are costly and difficult to standardize. There is certainly an opportunity for a comparative study between a medical simulation model and a standardized patient model. Currently, every allopathic medical student in the country is required to take the United States Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE) Step II Clinical Skills (CS) exam prior to receiving his or her medical license. There are only five testing centers in the U.S. and the exam is very costly. The use of a standardized medical simulation experience may lower costs overall and allow for a greater number of testing sites. This study had several limitations. First, it was a small pilot study with only 53 participants. A larger sample size may have yielded different results. Similarly, we only compared two simulation scenarios (ASA and TCA) versus two written examinations (ASA and TCA) using 10 knowledge-based questions. We chose two of the most important topics in emergency toxicology: tricyclic antidepressant and salicylate poisoning. These are low frequency, yet high morbidity events. These are two of the major learning objectives that will be relevant for patient care in Emergency Medicine. We estimated that two scenarios were adequate and feasible for an initial proof-of-concept study. There is also uncontrolled variability in the EMR previous experiences in medical simulation as well as medical toxicology. In addition, a senior EM resident on July 1 is obviously not as experienced in emergency medicine or medical toxicology as a senior resident at the end of the same academic year. However, this inter-learner variability would be expected to progress in both study groups similarly throughout the year. While the residents were blinded to the purpose of the study and the cases were confidential, there is a possibility that some residents discussed the cases outside of the rotation. Similarly, all assessments used by the American Board of Emergency Medicine and other specialty certifying organizations have to deal with variability in training and experience. In addition, the scoring was performed by a single physician (fourth author), which could introduce some bias. However, we feel that this design was an overall strength in our study put in place to eliminate inter-rater reliability and potentially reducing bias on the whole. In the future, it would be insightful to include more patient scenarios (sim versus written) among a larger pool of EMR. Similar results in simulation accuracy and precision as well as continued higher satisfaction, may justify the incorporation of medical simulation as an assessment tool as a trial in the ABEM certifying (oral) examination. As discussed previously, medical simulation may provide an avenue to assess procedural competency and certain American College of Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) core competencies (i.e., interpersonal and communication skills, professionalism, patient care, and systems-based practice) that cannot easily be assessed using the current ABEM certifying examination format. Other medical subspecialties might utilize medical simulation for similar reasons. Eventually, it would be ideal to quantify a real change in patient outcomes and/or patient satisfaction after an intervention with a simulation-based assessment tool versus a standard written examination. It should be pointed out, however, that simulation is merely a tool that can be used during assessment; simulation cannot replace direct faculty supervision during patient care. Conclusions In this pilot study, assessment by sim has similar accuracy (demonstrated by the similar mean scores) and precision (demonstrated by similar standard deviations) but higher satisfaction rates when compared to a written examination. Most participants felt simulation should be incorporated into the ABEM certifying (oral) examination. Acknowledgments Thank you to the residents who participated in this study. We would also like to thank those who attended and participated in the 2011 North American Congress of Clinical Toxicology (NACCT) Fellow-In-Training Research Symposium and the faculty and students of the 2011–2012 Scholars for Teaching Excellence Faculty Fellowship (STEFF) at the University of Illinois at Chicago for their valuable input. Special thanks to Errick Christian, MS, for his assistance with data management and analysis. Biography Clockwise, from top left: Michael R. Christian, MD, MSMA member since 2012, is Assistant Professor of Emergency Medicine and Pediatrics, University of Missouri-Kansas City School of Medicine, Department of Emergency Medicine, Truman Medical Center, Medical Toxicologist, Division of Clinical Pharmacology and Medical Toxicology. Michelle J. Sergel, MD, Mark B. Mycyk, MD, and Steven E. Aks, DO, are with the John H. Stroger, Jr. Hospital in Chicago, Ill. Email: michael.christian@tmcmed.org Open in a new tab Open in a new tab Open in a new tab Open in a new tab Footnotes Disclosure None reported. References 1. Boulet JR, Murray DJ. Simulation-based assessment in Anesthesiology: requirements for practical implementation. Anesthesiology. 2010;112(4):1041–1052. doi: 10.1097/ALN.0b013e3181cea265. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Kaye AR, Salud LH, Domont ZB, et al. Expanding the use of simulators as assessment tools: the new pop quiz. Stud Health Technol Inform. 2011;163:271–273. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Marco J, Holmes DR. Simulation: present and future roles. J Am Coll Cardiol Intv. 2008;1(5):590–592. doi: 10.1016/j.jcin.2008.08.015. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Crosby E. The role of simulator-based assessments in physician competency evaluations. Can J Anesth. 2010;57(7):627–635. doi: 10.1007/s12630-010-9323-3. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Decker S, Utterback VA, Thomas MB, Mitchell M, Sportsman S. Assessing continued competency through simulation: A call for stringent action. Nurs Educ Perspect. 2011;32(2):120–125. doi: 10.5480/1536-5026-32.2.120. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Nasca TJ, Philibert I, Brigham T, Flynn TC. The next GME accreditation system – rationale and benefits. New Engl J Med. 2012;366:1051–1056. doi: 10.1056/NEJMsr1200117. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. McGahie WC, Cohen ER, Wayne DB. Are United States Medical Licensing Exam Step 1 and 2 scores valid measures for postgraduate medical residency selection decisions? Acad Med. 2011;861(1):48–52. doi: 10.1097/ACM.0b013e3181ffacdb. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Bong CL, Lightdale JR, Fredette ME, Weinstock P. Effects of simulation versus traditional tutorial-based training on physiologic stress levels among clinicians: a pilot study. Simul Healthc. 2010;5(5):272–278. doi: 10.1097/SIH.0b013e3181e98b29. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Cook DA. One drop at a time: research to advance the science of simulation. Simul Healthc. 2010;5(1):1–4. doi: 10.1097/SIH.0b013e3181c82aaa. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Okuda Y, Bryson EO, DeMaria S, et al. The utility of simulation in medical education: what is the evidence? Mt Sinai J Med. 2009;76(4):330–343. doi: 10.1002/msj.20127. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Barsuk JH, Cohen ER, Potts S, et al. Dissemination of a simulation-based mastery learning intervention reduces central-line associated bloodstream infections. BMJ Qual Saf. 2014;0:1–8. doi: 10.1136/bmjqs-2013-002665. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Blum RH, Boulet JR, Cooper JB, Muret-Wagstaff SL. Simulation-based assessment to identify critical gaps in safe anesthesia resident performance. Anesthesiology. 2014;120:129–41. doi: 10.1097/ALN.0000000000000055. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Goo