dimirova jarnagin chemogenomics konkle kentner bielajew liguori ciurlionis ditewig doktor andracki gagne waring gopalakrishnan blomme cyp1a autoinduction a998679 macri pasquali bonsignore pieretti cirulli chiarotti laviola e1015 mccarroll keshava cimino dearfield keshava kligerman protzel putzrath schoeny spruijt zutphen baumans mering kaliste korhonen nevalainen moggs moulin pognan brees kammuller chibout moncek duncko jezova corrow husbandry tohei hokao shinoda kaolin pica 289d weichbrod cisar simmonds alvares ueng beddings microsomal bilbo neuroimmune dewitt fonken siberian hamsters','Abstract Environmental enrichment in rodents may improve animal well-being but can affect neurologic development, immune system function, and aging. We tested the hypothesis that wood block enrichment affects the interpretation of traditional and transcriptomic endpoints in an exploratory toxicology testing model using a well-characterized reference compound, cyclophosphamide. ANOVA was performed to distinguish effects of wood block enrichment separate from effects of 40 mg/kg cyclophosphamide treatment. Biologically relevant and statistically significant effects of wood block enrichment occurred only for body weight gain. ANOVA demonstrated the expected effects of cyclophosphamide on food consumption, spleen weight, and hematology. According to transcriptomic endpoints, cyclophosphamide induced fewer changes in gene expression in liver than in spleen. Splenic transcriptomic pathways affected by cyclophosphamide included: iron hemostasis; vascular tissue angiotensin system; hepatic stellate cell activation and fibrosis; complement activation; TGFβ-induced hypertrophy and fibrosis; monocytes, macrophages, and atherosclerosis; and platelet activation. Changes in these pathways due to cyclophosphamide treatment were consistent with bone marrow toxicity regardless of enrichment. In a second study, neither enrichment nor type of cage flooring altered body weight or food consumption over a 28-d period after the first week. In conclusion, wood block enrichment did not interfere with a typical exploratory toxicology study; the effects of ingested wood on drug level kinetics may require further consideration. Abbreviations: CYP, cytochrome P450; ROAST, Rosetta Array Search Tool The use of animals in safety assessment studies for pharmaceutical candidate compounds is intended to identify and characterize toxicity liabilities and to estimate the safety margin between efficacy and toxicity. These data support subsequent clinical trial design. In the United States, the use of animals for these studies is regulated by USDA Animal Welfare Regulations. 26 These regulations largely exclude rats, mice, and birds from their scope, but the Guide for the Care and Use of Laboratory Animals 22 provides recommendations for all species, including rodents. In the most recent revision of the Guide , new text specifically addresses the topic of environmental enrichment. 22 The Guide describes the role of environmental enrichment in animal husbandry and the potential benefits and risks of implementation without providing specific guidance for implementation. The European Union Commission recommended social enrichment for rodents and encouraged other forms of enrichment. 8 Enrichment is intended to enhance the psychologic wellbeing of animals, resulting in a greater ability to cope with environmental stressors through, or as evidenced by, increased species-typical behaviors. 24 Potential benefits of an effective enrichment program may include increased resistance to stressful environmental challenges, such as handling, restraint, injection, and blood sampling. 14 , 18 Effects of enrichment on neurologically relevant endpoints are described by many; 23 however, reports conflict regarding the effect of enrichment on body weight gain or organ weight. 3 , 19 , 24 Ideally, effective enrichment increases animal well-being (refinement) and reduces variability in key endpoint data, possibly permitting reductions in experimental group size. For example, moderate stress during neonatal development ultimately resulted in reduced stress in adult rats and decreased interindividual variability in neurobehavioral endpoints. 16 For toxicology testing, enrichment methods should, at a minimum, not increase variability in traditional endpoints and optimally would increase the translatability of the findings to other species. Although there may be a shift toward social housing, toxicology studies in the United States currently typically use individually housed rodents to facilitate the measurement of food consumption and identification of clinical signs referable to each animal. Therefore, we focused on a form of enrichment compatible with singly housed rats. For rats, environmental enrichment can consist of social housing, modifications to caging, or special feeding. 2 The application of social housing to regulated rodent toxicology studies has not been applied consistently across industry. However, modification of housing, such as the inclusion of bedding, nesting, and gnawing materials, to promote species-specific behaviors continues to gain popularity. To our knowledge, a prospective analysis of the effects of this kind of enrichment on traditional toxicology endpoints in rats and on newer technologies such as transcriptomics has not been reported. Because some husbandry conditions can alter hepatic enzyme activity, 27 consequently altering drug disposition and the interpretation of regulated toxicity studies, careful evaluation of enrichment effects is warranted. In addition to traditional endpoints (for example, body weight, organ weight, clinical pathology), microarray-based transcriptomic tools are now used to predict toxicity outcomes from short-duration studies. 5 The effects of enrichment on these molecular endpoints have not previously been reported and may be difficult to recognize. Short-duration (less than 2 wk) exploratory toxicology studies are used frequently in the pharmaceutical industry to assess toxicology risks with new compounds and to determine compound priority for further development efforts. These studies often incorporate sophisticated endpoints (transcriptomics) to predict toxic mechanisms from small groups of animals. 5 Minimized group size ( n = 3 to 5) is important for allowing these exploratory studies to be used early in the compound selection process due to the resource constraints of synthesizing new chemical entities. From exploratory study designs, it becomes possible to detect robust toxicologic effects, but with current technology, follow up studies using designs of greater statistical power are still required to detect subtle toxicologic effects. In this context, we sought to test the hypothesis that the use of wood block enrichment in rats individually housed in stainless steel wire-bottom caging in an exploratory toxicology testing paradigm alters the interpretation of its endpoints. The group size ( n = 3 to 5 rats) used in these studies has been found, in our practice, to be sufficient for detection of major toxicity liabilities. The goal of this investigation was to determine whether wood block enrichment in a subacute exploratory rat toxicology study alters the assessment of compound liabilities. To test the hypothesis, we chose a nonproprietary reference compound, cyclophosphamide, which causes detectable toxicity in traditional and transcriptomic endpoints and for which dose–response characteristics already are defined in existing literature. Cyclophosphamide is a well-characterized anticancer drug that is activated by cytochrome P450 (CYP) 2B1 and exerts its effects through alkylation of DNA in rapidly dividing cells. 17 Toxicity with cyclophosphamide is mainly bone marrow suppression, immunosuppression, and hemorrhagic cystitis. 11 In humans, cyclophosphamide treatment induces nausea and emesis; 11 in rats it has been reported to induce pica. 25 Cyclophosphamide offers the additional advantage that any changes in metabolic enzyme activity could alter the kinetics of its activation and therefore alter the interpretation of traditional and transcriptomic endpoints. For these reasons, we selected cyclophosphamide as a toxicant to test our hypothesis regarding wood block enrichment. Materials and Methods Animals. Male Sprague–Dawley rats (Crl:CD[SD]IGS BR) weighing approximately 250 g were obtained from Charles River Laboratories (Portage, MI). The rats received noncertified rodent chow and water ad libitum. Rats were housed 2 or 3 per cage for 2 d after receipt to aid in acclimation. Three experiments tested different aspects of the proposed hypothesis. In experiment 1 ( Table 1 ), we used a subacute toxicity study design with cyclophosphamide to test the effect of enrichment on the interpretation of endpoints. Rats were given either wood block enrichment (aspen wood; size, 1 × 1 × 4 cm; Ssniff, Soest, Germany) or no enrichment and were housed in stainless steel wire-bottom hanging cages. In each condition, one group received cyclophosphamide daily (40 mg/kg by oral gavage in saline), whereas the vehicle-only group received saline only. Treatment was for 5 d followed by overnight fast and necropsy. Multiple endpoints were evaluated, including body weight, liver and spleen weights, serum chemistry, hematology, gross findings, histopathology, and gene expression in liver and spleen. The terminal body weight was less than the day 5 weight because rats were fasted overnight prior to necropsy. Clinical observations were recorded daily. Food consumption and body weight gain were recorded over the interval spanning study days 1 through 5. Histologic examination was performed on samples of stomach, pancreas, liver, small and large intestines, heart, lung, kidney, thymus, spleen, and bone marrow that were prepared routinely and stained with hematoxylin and eosin. Table 1. Study design Experiment Treatment Drug dosage (mg/kg/d) Study duration (d) Wood block present? No. of rats Type of caging 1 Vehicle 0 5 No 3 Wire-bottom Cyclophosphamide 40 5 No 3 Wire-bottom Vehicle 0 5 Yes 3 Wire-bottom Cyclophosphamide 40 5 Yes 3 Wire-bottom 2 Naïve Rats 0 28 No 10 Solid-bottom Naïve Rats 0 28 No 10 Wire-bottom Naïve Rats 0 28 Yes 10 Solid-bottom Naïve Rats 0 28 Yes 10 Wire-bottom 3 ABT-102 50 Single dose No 5 Solid-bottom ABT-102 50 Single dose Yes 5 Solid-bottom Open in a new tab Experiment 2 ( Table 1 ) tested the effects of housing and wood blocks on food consumption and body weight gain. Four groups of 10 rats each were housed in solid-bottom cages containing hardwood bedding (Sani-Chips, PJ Murphy Forest Products, Montville, NJ) or in wire-bottom cages; both housing types were also tested with and without wood block enrichment. Food consumption was determined weekly, and body weight was measured daily for 5 d each week. Wood blocks were replaced as necessary. Solid-bottom housing with bedding was considered an additional enriching factor for this experiment. Experiment 3 ( Table 1 ) tested the effects of wood block access on the levels of a reference compound (ABT-102, CAS 808756-71-0) known to have enhanced absorption in the presence of food (data not shown). Rats ( n = 5 per group) were group-housed on solid-bottom caging with hardwood bedding (Sani-Chips, PJ Murphy Forest Products) and provided with hardwood blocks (catalog no. K3511, Bio-Serv, Frenchtown, NJ) 24 h prior to receiving ABT-102 (50 mg/kg by oral gavage). Blood was collected from a single tail incision at 1, 2, 4, 8, 12, and 24 h after dosing, and EDTA-treated plasma was frozen at −20 °C until analysis. All animal studies were reviewed and approved by AbbVie''s Lake County IACUC. Animal studies were conducted as part of an AAALAC-accredited program, and a veterinarian was available to ensure appropriate animal care. Rats were euthanized by exsanguination under isoflurane anesthesia. Tissues for gene expression profiling were frozen in liquid nitrogen and stored at −80 °C until analysis. Other tissues were fixed in formalin for histopathology. Gene expression analysis. Frozen liver and spleen (50 to 100 mg) were added immediately to 3 mL of Qiazol reagent (Qiagen, Valencia, CA) and homogenized (Polytron 300D, Brinkman Instruments, Westbury, NY). Total RNA was isolated by using chloroform extraction and nucleic acid precipitation with isopropanol. Pellets were washed with 75% ethanol and resuspended in molecular-biology–grade water. Nucleic acid concentration was determined by OD 260 :OD 280 ratio (SpectraMax, Molecular Devices, Sunnyvale, CA), and RNA integrity was evaluated by using a bioanalyzer (model 2100, Agilent Technologies, Foster City, CA). Microarray hybridization was performed according to the standard protocol provided by Affymetrix (Santa Clara, CA). Briefly, approximately 5 µg of total RNA was reverse-transcribed into cDNA by using a Superscript II Double-Strand cDNA synthesis kit (Invitrogen Life Technologies, Carlsbad, CA) according to the manufacturer''s instructions, with the exception of the primer. The primer used for the reverse-transcription reaction was a modified T7 primer with 24 thymidines at the 5′ end (5′ GGC CAG TGA ATT GTA ATA CGA CTC ACT ATA GGG AGG CGG(dT) 24 3′; Affymetrix). cDNA was purified by using phenol–chloroform–isoamyl alcohol extraction (Invitrogen Life Technologies) in spin columns (Qiagen) followed by ethanol precipitation. Purified cDNA was resuspended in molecular-biology–grade water and used to synthesize biotin-labeled cRNA (RNA Transcript Labeling Kit, Enzo Life Sciences, Farmindale, NY) according to the manufacturer''s instructions. Labeled cRNA was purified by using RNeasy kits (Qiagen), and the cRNA concentration quantified. Approximately 20 µg of cRNA was fragmented in a solution of 40 mM Tris–acetate (pH 8.1), 100 mM potassium acetate, and 30 mM magnesium acetate at 94 °C for 35 min. Samples were hybridized at 45 °C overnight to a rat genome array containing approximately 31,000 transcripts (RG 230 2.0, Affymetrix) by using a hybridization oven (model 640, Affymetrix). Arrays subsequently were washed, stained twice with streptavidin–phycoerythrin (Molecular Probes, Carlsbad, CA) in an automated staining station (GeneChip Fluidics Workstation 400, Affymetrix) and then scanned (GeneChip Scanner 3000, Affymetrix). For RT-PCR of Cyp2b1/2, RNA samples were diluted to 20 ng/µL in molecular-biology–grade water. Samples (2.5 µL) were pipetted in duplicate into a 96-well optical reaction plate (Applied Biosystems, Carlsbad, CA); 17.5 µL of RT-PCR master mix (TaqMan EZ RT-PCR kit, Applied Biosystems) and Cyp2b1/2 primer probe sets (IDT Technologies, Coralville, IA) were added to each well. RT-PCR primer and probe sequences were: Cyp2b1/2 forward, 5′ GAG CTG TTC TAC CGG ACC TTT TC 3′; Cyp2b1/2 reverse, 5′ AGT ATT TCA GGA ACC CAG AGA A­GA ACT 3′; Cyp2b1/2 probe, 5′ TCC TAA GTT CAT TCT CC 3′; 28S rRNA forward, 5′ TTC ACC AAG CGT TGG ATT GTT 3′; 28S rRNA reverse, 5′ TGT CTG AAC CTG CGG TTC CT 3′; and 28S rRNA probe, 5′ TCA CGA CGG TCT AAA CCC AGC TCA CG 3′. RT-PCR was performed (ABI Prism 7900, Applied Biosystems) according to the following parameters: 2 min at 50 °C, 30 min at 60 °C, 5 min at 95 °C, and 40 cycles consisting of 20 s melting at 94 °C then 1 min annealing–extending at 62 °C. Results were analyzed by using the ΔΔCt method as previously described. 15 Results are expressed as fold change relative to values for vehicle-only controls. Pharmacokinetic analysis. Plasma was spiked with internal standard, and then compounds were selectively removed by using liquid-liquid extraction with methyl tert-butyl ether at neutral pH. The test article and internal standard were separated from each other and from coextracted contaminants on a silica column (50 × 3 mm, Luna CN 5 μm, Phenomenex, Torrance, CA) with a mobile phase consisting of 50:50 acetonitrile:0.1% trifluoroacetic acid at a flow rate of 0.4 mL/min. Analysis was performed on an API2000 instrument (PE Sciex Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA) with a turbo-ionspray interface by using multiple reaction monitoring. Peak areas of the test article and internal standard were determined by using MacQuan software (Sciex). The concentration of each sample was interpolated from a standard curve that was calculated by least-squares linear-regression analysis of the peak area ratio (test article peak area divided by internal standard peak area) versus test article concentration of standard dilutions in rat plasma. Pharmacokinetic analysis of the plasma concentrations from individual rats was performed by using WinNonlin (Pharsight, Sunnyvale, CA). Statistics. In experiment 1, a two-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) using factors of compound treatment (cyclophosphamide or vehicle) and enrichment (wood block enriched or unenriched) was calculated for each endpoint. Results are given as p-values listed at the bottom of Tables 2 through 6 . Effect of wood block enrichment was tested; bolded values indicate significance ( P ≤ 0.05). Treatment-enrichment interactions were also modeled. Liver and spleen weights were normalized by total body weight. Reference intervals consisting of the 5% to 95% confidence interval from the previous year''s vehicle-only data ( n = 133 for clinical pathology, n = 160 for organ weights) were used as guidance for biological significance of changes in individual animal data. In experiment 2, we tested enrichment effects on body weight and food consumption using a mixed effect model where caging (wire and solid bottom) and enrichment (wood block enriched or unenriched) were fixed effects, and time of measurement was a random effect. A caging-enrichment interaction term was included in the model. Additionally, a model was fit with time as a fixed effect to test body weight and food consumption for differences at the 4 wk time point. In Figure 1 A , the 5 percentile to 95 percentile interval for vehicle controls on wire-bottom caging is reported. In experiment 3, Student''s t -test assessed the significance of wood block enrichment on the individually estimated area under the curve (AUC) for each rat. Cyp2b1/2 fold change values from individual rats were calculated from RT-PCR data, and 2-tailed t- test was conducted. The effect of enrichment on Cyp2b1/2 induction by cyclophosphamide treatment over vehicle-only control values was tested by using linear- and log-transformed data. Table 2. Experiment 1: Body weight and food consumption Body weight Food consumption (g/ 4 d) Treatment Rat no. Day 1 (g) Day 5 (g) Gain (%) Vehicle + no wood block 1001 311.7 321.0 3.0 100.2 1003 304.8 315.0 3.3 116.5 1005 330.1 345.9 4.8 121.9 Mean 315.5 327.3 3.7 112.9 SEM 7.6 9.5 0.6 6.5 Cyclophosphamide + no wood block 2001 302.0 300.7 −0.4 81.9 2003 314.4 308.3 −1.9 85.4 2005 331.4 333.3 0.6 90.8 Mean 315.9 314.1 −0.6 86.0 SEM 8.5 9.8 0.7 2.6 Vehicle + wood block 3001 318.1 311.8 −2.0 118.9 3003 332.0 317.0 −4.5 110.1 3005 334.4 325.4 −2.7 106.2 Mean 328.2 318.1 −3.1 111.7 SEM 5.1 4.0 0.8 3.8 Cyclophosphamide + wood block 4001 326.5 293.4 −10.1 63.3 4003 351.3 358.7 2.1 111.7 4005 314.5 289.2 −8.0 75.0 Mean 330.8 313.8 −5.4 83.3 SEM 10.8 22.5 3.8 14.6 p-value 0.019 0.823 Open in a new tab P values from ANOVA testing indicate effect of enrichment regardless of vehicle or cyclophosphamide treatment; bolded values considered significant ( P ≤ 0.05). Table 6. Experiment 1: Hemogram Treatment Rat no. RBC (10 12 /L) Hgb (g/dL) Hct (%) MCV (fL) MCH (pg) MCHC (g/dL) Platelets (×10 9 /L) Mean platelet volume (fL) Reticulocytes (10 12 /L) Vehicle + no wood block 1001 7.11 13.7 45.7 64.3 19.3 30.1 ↓ 1263 9.1 0.448 was new number that I got 1003 7.13 14.7 48.2 67.7 20.6 30.5 1111 9.2 492 1005 6.89 13.4 42.0 60.9 19.5 32.0 1317 8.7 476 Mean 7.04 13.9 45.3 64.3 19.8 30.9 1230 9.0 472 SEM 0.08 0.4 1.8 2.0 0.4 0.6 62 0.2 13 Cyclophosphamide + no wood block 2001 5.68 ↓ 11.0 ↓ 34.2 ↓ 60.3 19.4 32.2 397 ↓ 8.6 5 ↓ 