k 3001 5.85 0.71 4.95 0.12 ↓ 0.08 ↑ 3003 n/d n/d n/d n/d n/d 3005 10.88 1.93 8.63 0.20 0.09 ↑ Mean 8.37 1.32 6.79 0.16 0.09 Cyclophosphamide + wood block 4001 0.20 ↓ 0.11↓ 0.07 ↓ 0.00 ↓ 0.01 4003 n/d n/d n/d n/d n/d 4005 0.29 ↓ 0.03 ↓ 0.26 ↓ 0.00 ↓ 0.00 Mean 0.25 0.07 0.17 0.00 0.01 5%–95% CI 5.30–13.28 0.55–2.13 3.59–10.68 0.13–1.64 0.00–0.04 p-value 0.670 0.602 0.507 0.293 0.107 Open in a new tab P values from ANOVA testing indicate effect of enrichment regardless of vehicle or cyclophosphamide treatment; bolded values considered significant ( P ≤ 0.05). Arrows indicate deviation from 5% to 95% confidence interval (CI). Microarray analysis. For microarray analysis, the scanned image and intensity files were imported into gene expression analysis software (version 7.2.2.0, Resolver, Rosetta Biosoftware, Seattle, WA). The microarray data have been deposited in National Center for Biotechnology Information Gene Expression Omnibus and are accessible through GEO Series accession number GSE48407 . Unless otherwise specified, individual gene expression ratios were built for each treatment rat by comparing to the averaged vehicle controls, matched for enrichment, by using the Rosetta Resolver proprietary error model. Genes and experimental treatments were grouped for visualization by agglomerative hierarchical clustering by using the Pearson correlation, as described in each figure. Unless otherwise specified, all significant changes were filtered by the criteria of a minimum 2-fold change and significance of P ≤ 0.05 (by the Rosetta error model). The Rosetta Resolver statistical model identifies individual expression changes that are significantly different for an individual animal when compared with the average of the control set. This P value does not indicate that changed genes are a significant effect of treatment, which is a between-groups comparison. Experiment 1 was not powered sufficiently to allow between-group comparisons on a gene-by-gene basis for all modulated genes when corrections for multiple comparisons are used. To analyze between-groups treatment effects from gene expression data, we used the Rosetta Array Search Tool (ROAST; Rosetta Biosoftware) and Ingenuity Pathway Analysis methods. ROAST compares a query item against multiple target items to rank target items that are most similar to the query item. ROAST was performed against 346 liver and 34 spleen toxicants in the Iconix DrugMatrix database that had been dosed for 5 d at one or more dose levels. A list of ROAST correlation coefficients was generated for each reference toxicant by correlating the gene expression profiles of cyclophosphamide-treated unenriched rats with those of vehicle-treated unenriched rats. A second list of correlation coefficients was generated for enriched cyclophosphamide-treated rats compared with enriched vehicle-treated rats. The 29 most highly correlated reference profiles (first cut) were combined into lists for each enrichment category, and the top 10 were selected (second cut). For liver, because not all of the top 29 correlating reference profiles were similar across rats, the number of rats matching a reference compound was noted, and the average correlation metric was derived only from those animals. Functional pathway analyses were generated (version 14197757, Pathway Analysis Software, Ingenuity, Redwood City, CA; www.ingenuity.com ) by using a filter of 2-fold change in expression levels and a P value of 0.05 or less, unless otherwise specified. The DrugMatrix v5.0 pathways were imported and analyzed in Ingenuity. The software returns a percentage target intersection, which is the proportion of modulated genes within a pathway, and a P value, which is the probability that modulation of the pathway gene set was due to random chance. Pathways were ranked by most significant P value, and the top 20 pathways, ranked by lowest P value among treatment conditions, were compared between enriched and unenriched conditions. Gene ontology analysis was performed by using the Rosetta Resolver algorithm on the gene expression profiles of liver and spleen from vehicle-treated rats provided access to wood block enrichment relative to those of vehicle-treated rats not provided wood block enrichment. Statistical cutoffs for gene expression changes (separated as up- and downregulated) used in the gene ontology analysis was fold change of 2.0 or greater and a P value of 0.01 or less in at least 2 of 3 rats. Biologic processes with gene ontology numbers of 3 or less were extracted. Of these biologic processes, profiles with 2 or more changed gene members and with at least 1% of the biologic process changed were extracted and listed. Results Individual animal data for experiment 1 are presented in Tables 2 through 6 . ANOVA across multiple endpoints for effects of wood block enrichment independent of cyclophosphamide treatment found significance (p-value ≤0.05) for body weight, liver weight, serum sodium, and serum calcium. Serum sodium and calcium were within historical reference ranges and therefore do not represent a biologically significant change. With the exception of one vehicle control rat, relative liver weight was within historical reference range as well. Therefore, our analysis of the effects of wood block enrichment on traditional endpoints of an exploratory toxicology study found only body weight to be biologically and statistically significantly affected by wood block enrichment. In experiment 1, cyclophosphamide treatment, regardless of wood block enrichment, was analyzed by ANOVA and had significant effects on the following parameters ( P ≤ 0.05): food consumption, spleen weight, WBC, neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, RBC, hemoglobin, hematocrit, mean cell volume, mean corpuscular hemoglobin, platelets, mean platelet volume, total protein, albumin, AST, GLDH, ALKP, ALT, and cholesterol. Histopathologic changes were noted only in rats dosed with cyclophosphamide and were identical regardless of wood block treatment: marked decreased bone marrow hematopoiesis and lymphoid depletion of spleen (moderate) and thymus (marked). Experiment 2 was conducted to determine whether wood block enrichment altered growth rate or food consumption and was initiated because of the findings in experiment 1. Growth curves for rats with and without wood block enrichment on solid-bottom caging and with wood block enrichment on wire-bottom caging were within the 5%-to-95% confidence interval derived from the wire-bottom, unenriched group ( Figure 1 A ). Food consumption fell ( P ≤ 0.01) transiently in the group without wood block enrichment and housed in solid-bottom cages when compared with rats housed in solid-bottom caging with bedding and wood block enrichment, or in wire-bottom caging without wood block enrichment. This effect occurred only during the first week; no significant effects of housing or enrichment were detected for the remainder of the study ( Figure 1 B ). Experiment 3 was conducted to determine whether wood block enrichment alters the pharmacokinetics of a compound known to have enhanced absorption with food. In a preliminary study (not reported here), socially housed rats were given wood block enrichment immediately before dosing, and rats did not gnaw on wood blocks. In experiment 3, rats were singly housed and divided into groups with or without wood block enrichment prior to pharmacokinetic analysis of ABT-102. Rats gnawed on wood blocks, but no wood particles could be identified in the stomach or cecum at necropsy (24 h after dosing). Drug exposure was equivalent ( P = 0.11) between rats that received wood block enrichment and controls without enrichment ( Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Concentration (mean ± SEM; n = 5) of ABT-102 in the plasma of rats after a single oral dose of 50 mg/kg. Comparison of AUC of individual rats by enrichment category did not reveal a significant difference. In addition to the traditional toxicological endpoints examined in experiment 1, we analyzed gene expression in the livers and spleens of all rats. A heatmap showing the effect of enrichment on gene expression in vehicle controls is shown in Figure 3 . Genes whose expression was altered greater than 3-fold in liver and spleen are listed in Table 7 . In liver, of the 67 probe changes (0.2% of the microarray), very few were consistently changed in rats given enrichment. In spleen, a change of at least 2-fold between vehicle groups with and without enrichment was seen in 107 probe sets (0.3% of the microarray). No genes in spleen were identified that were upregulated more than 4-fold. Manual review of individual genes which were altered due to exposure to wood block enrichment in the liver and spleen did not reveal trends pointing to a particular biologic process. Therefore, we performed a more exhaustive analysis by using a gene ontology algorithm in the Rosetta Resolver software. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Effect of wood block enrichment of vehicle-treated rats on liver and spleen gene expression (filtered for fold-change greater than or equal to 2 and P ≤ 0.01 for probe sets changed in at least 2 of 3 rats). Each row represents the gene expression of one animal with wood block enrichment relative to averaged gene expression of rats without enrichment. In liver, the total number of gene changes is 67, which is 0.2% of the microarray. In spleen, the total number of gene changes is 107, which is 0.3% of the microarray. Table 7. Experiment 1: Genes modulated by wood block enrichment Tissue Modulation Sequence Sequence description Average fold change Liver Downregulated LOC685020 Similar to paired immunoglobin-like type 2 receptor α −8.0 Nr0b2 Nuclear receptor subfamily 0, group B, member 2 −6.3 Rgs16 Regulator of G-protein signaling 16 −4.2 Ccnd1 Cyclin D1 −4.1 Zfp354a Zinc finger protein 354A −4.0 EST Sequence ID 1390731_at −3.9 Ppp1r3b Protein phosphatase 1, regulatory (inhibitor) subunit 3B −3.4 EST 