CR results for Cyp2b1/2 mRNA levels in rat liver after treatment with cyclophosphamide in the presence or absence of wood block enrichment relative to respective vehicle-treated control. Error bars represent 1 SD of technical duplicates. The microarray analysis showed more changes in gene expression in spleen than in liver, and visual inspection of the heatmap shows that similarity between rats within identical treatment groups is higher in spleen than in liver, regardless of wood block enrichment. We used ROAST to compare the gene expression profiles of cyclophosphamide-treated rats with the average gene expression of vehicle controls, grouped by enrichment. Within enrichment categories, average liver gene expression correlation (where 1 is a perfect correlation) was 0.56 and 0.47 for rats without and with enrichment, respectively. Average spleen gene expression correlation was 0.93 and 0.91 for rats without and with enrichment, respectively. For between enrichment categories, correlation was 0.34 and 0.88 for liver and spleen, respectively. The effects of cyclophosphamide treatment on splenic gene expression were more consistent between rats, regardless of enrichment, than were those in liver. Overlap of regulated annotated genes according to enrichment status is presented in Figure 6 . In liver, only 17% of modulated genes are in common between rats with and without wood block enrichment. In contrast, spleen gene expression data show that 58% of all modulated genes are in common between groups with and without enrichment. To put the liver gene expression findings in context, we randomly selected 2 sets of 3 liver gene expression profiles per set from unrelated vehicle experiments in a library of 243 profiles and analyzed them as done previously. We repeated the random sampling 9 additional times and obtained an overlap of 14.7% ± 1.3% (mean ± SEM) and an average set size of 56 genes, indicating that the observed 17% overlap of modulated genes in the livers of rats with and without wood block enrichment is consistent with treatment-unrelated biologic variation. Figure 6. Open in a new tab Venn diagram of modulated genes similar between rats with and without wood block enrichment for liver and spleen; genes are relative to vehicle-only control with identical enrichment. Liver and spleen circles are in relative proportion to each other. Overlap in hepatic gene expression (17%) is much less than that in spleen (58%). Data are filtered for probe sets with a fold-change greater than or equal to 2 and P ≤ 0.01 in at least 2 of 3 animals. Another approach to transcriptomic analysis in toxicology is the classification of compounds by the similarity of their gene expression profiles, with the expectation that compounds activating similar pathways of toxicity will have similar gene expression profiles. We compared cyclophosphamide treatment effects on liver and spleen gene expression with liver and spleen gene expression profiles from rat 5-d repeat-dosing studies in the Iconix DrugMatrix database. 12 The top 10 matching reference profiles, ranked by correlation metric, are listed in Table 9 . In liver, profiles included antineoplastic or immunomodulatory agents (doxorubicin, epirubicin, leflunomide, azathioprine), NSAID (balsalazide, sulindac, ketorolac), and endocrine-active compounds (progesterone, clomiphene). In spleen, profiles included alkylating agents (ifosfamide, procarbazine, lomustine, busulfan), DNA crosslinking agents (mitomycin C, cisplatin) and other antineoplastic and immunomodulatory agents (thioguanine, leflunomide). Table 9. Experiment 1: Comparison of cyclophosphamide-induced gene expression correlations to reference compounds with and without wood block enrichment Correlation metric (no. of rats) Tissue Drug matrix reference profile Without wood block With wood block Liver Epirubicin (2.7 mg/kg) 0.300 (2) 0.182 (3) Benzothiazyl (2600 mg/kg) 0.291 (2) 0.197 (1) Leflunomide (60 mg/kg) 0.282 (2) 0.193 (2) Indomethacin (12 mg/kg) 0.272 (1) (0) Clotrimazole (52 mg/kg) 0.268 (1) (0) Balsalazide (1100 mg/kg) 0.260 (1) (0) Sulindac (132 mg/kg) 0.259 (2) (0) Doxorubicin (3 mg/kg) 0.256 (3) 0.206 (3) Ketorolac (48 mg/kg) 0.255 (2) 0.202 (2) Spironolactone (300 mg/kg) 0.254 (1) 0.132 (1) Chloroform (600 mg/kg) 0.252 (2) 0.194 (3) Azathioprine (54 mg/kg) 0.201 (1) 0.191 (1) Clomiphene (250 mg/kg) (0) 0.184 (2) Progesterone (11.3 mg/kg) (0) 0.182 (1) Spleen Ifosfamide (143 mg/kg) 0.456 (3) 0.424 (3) Mitomycin C (1.7 mg/kg) 0.335 (3) 0.323 (3) Leflunomide (60 mg/kg) 0.307 (3) 0.281 (3) Procarbazine (54 mg/kg) 0.292 (3) 0.236 (3) Doxorubicin (3 mg/kg) 0.291 (3) 0.262 (3) Lomustine (8.75 mg/kg) 0.289 (3) 0.241 (3) Cisplatin (2 mg/kg) 0.257 (3) 0.198 (3) Thioguanine (24 mg/kg) 0.254 (3) 0.179 (3) Busulfan (9 mg/kg) 0.249 (3) 0.191 (3) Diethylstilbestrol (280 mg/kg) 0.243 (3) 0.169 (3) Open in a new tab For liver gene expression reference profiles, not all rats had the same matching profiles within the top 29 matches (first cut). The number of rats contributing to the average correlation metric is given in parentheses. Pathway analysis is a method to simplify the complexity of gene expression data. Predefined biochemical pathways from the Iconix DrugMatrix database were analyzed for magnitude of perturbation by cyclophosphamide treatment in the presence or absence of wood block enrichment on a group-wise basis. The top 20 pathways for both liver and spleen are listed in Table 10 .In liver, 16.7% of the genes in the Hemes from Protoporphyrin IX pathway were regulated depending on enrichment status. Cholesterol biosynthesis and lipid transport pathways had more than 10% of member genes regulated in the absence of enrichment, but no significant regulation was noted in the presence of enrichment. For the remaining pathways with significant gene modulation, fewer than 10% of genes in the pathway were regulated, indicating a lack of coordinated regulation for those pathways. In spleen, all pathways in the top 20 had more than 10% of genes regulated, with the Iron Hemostasis pathway having 37% of member genes regulated regardless of the availability of enrichment. The commonality of the splenic pathways includes iron and heme synthesis, macrophage-related pathways, and TGFβ–tissue remodeling pathways. Table 10. Experiment 1: Comparison of cyclophosphamide-induced pathway modulation with and without wood block enrichment No wood block Wood block Tissue Set name (no. of genes in pathway) P % modulated P % modulated Liver Hemes from protoporphyrin IX (36) 9.09E–08 16.7 6.28E–09 16.7 PPARα and fatty acid metabolism (65) 5.47E–05 7.7 Integrin signaling (336) 6.22E–01 0.6 6.35E–05 2.4 AMP dinase: a metabolic master switch (74) 1.02E–04 6.8 2.56E–01 1.4 Bcr–Abl signaling (Gleevec action) (119) 5.26E–01 0.8 1.18E–04 4.2 B-cell receptor signaling (205) 3.66E–01 1.0 1.79E–04 2.9 NF-κB signaling (93) 2.99E–04 5.4 5.35E–04 4.3 p53 signaling (81) 3.16E–04 4.9 Complement activation (55) 3.90E–04 7.3 2.04E–02 3.6 Cell cycle G1/S transition (86) 4.17E–01 1.2 3.97E–04 4.7 Hepatic stellate cell activation and fibrosis (157) 7.55E–02 1.9 4.27E–04 3.2 Transportation of lipids (27) 6.26E–04 11.1 Oxidative stress (113) 7.30E–04 4.4 1.05E–02 2.7 Interleukin 1 (IL1) signaling (102) 3.88E–03 3.9 7.57E–04 3.9 Cholesterol biosynthesis (29) 7.75E–04 10.3 TGFβ signaling (108) 4.92E–01 0.9 9.37E–04 3.7 Renal injury: TGFβ-induced hypertrophy and fibrosis (124) 1.56E–03 3.2 Monocytes, macrophages, and atherosclerosis (80) 1.60E–03 5.0 4.07E–02 2.5 LPS- and IL1-induced changes in lipid metabolism (80) 1.60E–03 5.0 2.74E–01 1.3 Iron homeostasis (59) 3.09E–01 1.7 1.71E–03 5.1 Spleen Iron homeostasis (59) 6.02E–10 37.3 8.15E–11 37.3 Vascular tissue angiotensin system (177) 3.65E–07 20.3 2.37E–09 21.5 Hepatic stellate cell activation and fibrosis (157) 1.22E–05 19.1 3.53E–09 22.3 Complement activation (55) 1.19E–06 30.9 4.54E–08 32.7 Matrix metalloproteinases (MMP) and their regulation (68) 3.60E–04 22.1 3.25E–07 27.9 Renal injury: TGFβ-induced hypertrophy and fibrosis (124) 8.19E–06 21.0 3.45E–07 21.8 Cell cycle: the spindle checkpoint (82) 5.30E–07 26.8 7.32E–03 15.9 Monocytes, macrophages, and atherosclerosis (80) 7.38E–05 22.5 4.75E–06 23.8 TGFβ signaling (108) 6.33E–06 22.2 1.26E–04 18.5 Protoporphyrin IX biosynthesis (24) 9.63E–06 41.7 1.40E–03 29.2 Angiotensin II and cardiac hypertrophy (183) 2.31E–03 14.8 1.37E–05 16.9 Adiponectin and atherosclerosis (84) 4.44E–04 20.2 3.75E–05 21.4 Platelet activation (86) 6.14E–05 22.1 5.21E–05 20.9 G-protein G12 signaling (52) 9.24E–03 19.2 8.35E–05 25.0 Integrin signaling (336) 1.25E–02 11.9 9.36E–05 13.4 Renal injury: heme and heme oxygenase (48) 1.53E–03 22.9 1.55E–04 25.0 Phospholipid biosynthesis and remodeling (54) 1.20E–02 18.5 5.04E–04 22.2 Xenobiotic metabolism (86) 1.06E–02 16.3 5.47E–04 18.6 LXR and adipocyte differentiation (88) 7.78E–04 19.3 2.07E–01 10.2 Angiotensin-II–regulated cardiac hypertrophy and myocyte apoptosis (99) 1.16E–03 18.2 9.66E–04 17.2 Open in a new tab Pathways comprise multiple genes. The percentage of genes that were modulated in each pathway is given. Discussion The introduction of new practices to pharmaceutical safety testing optimally would increase efficiency in the selection of effective compounds and decrease safety risks. In addition to humane benefits, improvements in animal husbandry practices may reduce variability in study data, increasing statistical power; however enrichment must not negatively affect the interpretation of efficacy and safety data. Environmental enrichment in rodent housing affects neurologic development, 23 immune system function, 4 , 13 , 29 response to viral infection, 9 and aging. 1 Since modern toxicology has become more focused on the molecular pathways of toxicity, 6 , 20 the effect of environmental enrichment on modern toxicologic assessments cannot be assumed to be trivial. Therefore, we hypothesized that environmental enrichment of rats used in pharmaceutical safety assessment would alter the interpretation of a subacute repeated-dose toxicity experiment using the reference compound cyclophosphamide. We tested the hypothesis by using our exploratory toxicology paradigm, with 3 male rats per group and a focused set of endpoints. Our intent was to understand the effect of wood block enrichment on our interpretation of studies as currently performed and not to detect minimal effects that do not alter decision-making. In our experience, the group sizes ( n = 3 to 5) used for these experiments are sufficient to detect major toxicity risks. Cyclophosphamide was chosen as the reference compound because it has robust effects on several traditional toxicologic endpoints and alters gene expression in lymphoid organs. 11 Wood blocks for gnawing were chosen as the form of enrichment because they are appropriate for rats, are low-cost, and are easy to implement. Other forms of enrichment have been described for rats, including wheels, foods, climbing apparatuses, and group housing. 7 , 21 These other forms of enrichment may require additional study to determine their effects on findings from toxicology studies. Among the traditional toxicologic endpoints tested, only body weight was statistically and biologically changed by wood block enrichment in this study. Environmental enrichment has been associated with decreased 21 and increased 14 body weight gain, although some studies lack weight effects. 10 Although our facility has since adopted solid-bottom, bedded caging, because we found altered body weight gain in our initial experiment, we followed with studies to evaluate body weight gain and food consumption in rats housed with or without wood block-enrichment under the following housing conditions: wire-bottom flooring without bedding, and solid-bottom caging with bedding. In contrast to our initial results, subsequent studies with larger group sizes failed to replicate any significant effect of either enrichment or housing on body weight gain. We conclude that wood block enrichment does not affect weight gain under these 2 housing conditions. The effects of enrichment on body weight gain did not alter the cyclophosphamide-induced effect on body weight loss. The differences in traditional endpoints after wood block enrichment did not alter risk assessment in the context of a subacute exploratory toxicology study. We considered that absorption kinetics might be altered in rats exposed to wood blocks if the animals ingested sufficient quantities of wood, given that it could bind to the compound or alter digestion. The test compound used in this case, ABT-102, is known to have altered absorption in the presence of food compared with the fasted state (data not shown). However, rats exposed to wood blocks, even when fasted, did not consume wood to the degree that absorption was altered. In one preliminary study, group-housed rats disregarded wood blocks presented as a novel enrichment, but in other studies, group-housed rats did gnaw on wood blocks (data not shown). The interaction of animals with enrichment items should guide the strategy for their effective implementation. Gene expression of liver and spleen in vehicle-treated rats was analyzed to determine the effects of enrichment on the transcriptome. The genes with the greatest enrichment-associated modulation in liver included those involved in cytokine signaling, immunoregulation, and the cell cycle. In spleen, albumin was among the genes downregulated with enrichment; this finding underscores the need to interpret gene expression findings in the context of tissue and pathways. Gene ontology analysis was performed to rank-order changed biologic processes in liver and spleen; analysis was limited to higher level ontologies. In several processes, only 1 to 3 genes were modulated, and no process was modulated robustly. The gene ontology analysis suggests that the gene expression differences between vehicle-only groups are fluctuations due to factors other than enrichment (that is random experimental noise). Cyp2b1/2 mRNA analysis by RT-PCR, important for the reference compound chosen, was not induced in any group relative to unenriched vehicle controls. Analysis of cyclophosphamide-induced changes in gene expression revealed much greater consistency in spleen than liver. This finding is not surprising, given that cyclophosphamide targets hematopoietic tissue, which is present in the spleen but largely absent from the liver of 8-wk-old rats (as we used). Therefore our findings of high concordance in spleen gene expression, as analyzed by cluster and pathway analysis, are consistent with a toxicologically relevant mechanism in this tissue. One predominant pathway, iron homeostasis, may be related to treatment-related reductions of hematopoietic tissue in the spleen. Other pathways affected involve the innate immune response (macrophage activation, complement, platelet activation), which may be related to the lymphoid depletion in the spleen with relative sparing of the red pulp components. The interpretation of expression profiles and pathway analysis in spleen was not affected by the presence of wood block enrichment. In contrast, liver gene expression changes in rats dosed with cyclophosphamide did not correlate strongly with treatment. Pathway analysis of liver gene expression identified metabolic pathways (fatty acid metabolism, transport of lipids, and cholesterol biosynthesis) that were altered only in the absence of wood blocks. This finding may be attributable to the greater difference in weight loss observed between cyclophosphamide-treated and vehicle-control groups that did not receive wood block enrichment. The correlation analysis with Iconix DrugMatrix reference toxicants identified other antineoplastic and immunomodulatory compounds in both liver and spleen, but the correlation was more consistent in the spleen. Although the DrugMatrix reference set is much smaller for spleen, the similarity of cyclophosphamide-induced changes in splenic gene expression between enrichment categories ( Figure 6 ) suggests this consistency likely arises because the spleen is a histologic target organ for cyclophosphamide, whereas the liver is not. This examination of the effects of environmental enrichment on traditional and selected molecular-toxicology endpoints was limited to a single dose level of cyclophosphamide for 5 days. Because cyclophosphamide toxicity does not occur through neurologic mechanisms, we did not test the effects of enrichment on seizure, tremor, and similar neurologic endpoints. Enrichment may affect immune system function, 28 but the risk assessment of cyclophosphamide was not altered demonstrably in the traditional and transcriptomic endpoints examined here. Ingestion of wood particles, and its subsequent effects on drug absorption, may be dependent on behavioral responses to toxicity (that is, pica), which do not occur in single-, low-dose pharmacokinetic analysis. Cyclophosphamide induces pica in some strains of rats at doses comparable to those we used here. 25 Pica may be a characteristic response in rats to compounds that cause emesis in other species. Additional study of the effects of wood block enrichment on drug absorption may be justified when the ingestion of wood particles is expected or observed but otherwise appears unnecessary. In summary, we hypothesized that environmental enrichment through the use of wood blocks for gnawing would alter the interpretation of traditional and transcriptomic endpoints of a subacute toxicology testing paradigm. The evidence collected did not support this hypothesis with regard to the type of enrichment and the acute risk assessment tested here. Conversely, interanimal variation was not decreased noticeably with wood block enrichment, which therefore did not provide analytical benefits for a small, exploratory study design. The enhanced species-typical behavior of gnawing that is observed with wood-block enrichment in single-housing conditions provides rationale for using this form of enrichment in toxicology studies. 2 Acknowledgments AD, NB, DD, PE, PL, ML, JW, HY and WB are employees of AbbVie and may own AbbVie stock. BD was an employee of AbbVie at the time these data were collected. The design, study conduct, and financial support for these studies was provided by AbbVie. AbbVie participated in the interpretation of data, review, and approval of the manuscript. References 1. Arranz L, De Castro NM, Baeza I, Mate I, Viveros MP, De la Fuente M. 2010. Environmental enrichment improves age-related immune system impairment: long-term exposure since adulthood increases lifespan in mice. Rejuvenation Res 13:415–428 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Baumans V. 2005. Environmental enrichment for laboratory rodents and rabbits: requirements of rodents, rabbits, and research. ILAR J 46:162–170 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Bayne K. 2005. Potential for unintended consequences of environmental enrichment for laboratory animals and research results. ILAR J 46:129–139 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Benaroya-Milshtein N, Apter A, Yaniv I, Kukulansky T, Raz N, Haberman Y, Halpert H, Pick CG, Hollander N. 2007. Environmental enrichment augments the efficacy of idiotype vaccination for B-cell lymphoma. J Immunother 30:517–522 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Blomme EA, Yang Y, Waring JF. 2009. Use of toxicogenomics to understand mechanisms of drug-induced hepatotoxicity during drug discovery and development. Toxicol Lett 186:22–31 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Chen M, Zhang M, Borlak J, Tong W. 2012. A decade of toxicogenomic research and its contribution to toxicological science. Toxicol Sci 130:217–228 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Chmiel DJ, Jr, Noonan M. 1996. Preference of laboratory rats for potentially enriching stimulus objects. Lab Anim 30:97–101 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 